hadiths[605]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1816","From Utba ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Ibn Abi Khalid: he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Three shall recline upon dunes of musk on the Day of Resurrection in Paradise: a man who called to the five prayers by day and night seeking thereby the Face of Allah, a man who learned the Book of Allah and led a people with it while they are pleased with him, and an owned slave whom the bondage of this world did not distract from obedience to Allah."
"1817","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir: he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Hasten to the adhan, and do not hasten to the imamate. And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Hasten the imamate in the adhan, to pass beyond it."
"1818","From Israil, from Thuwayr ibn Abi Fakhita, from his father: he said: Ali ibn Abi Talib said: If you are able not to lead anyone, do so, for the imam — if only he knew what is upon him — would not lead. Or something like that; he mentioned something."
"1819","From Ibn Uyayna, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Abu Mamar: he said: The prayer was established and the people pushed it to one another, so Hudhayfa said: You shall appoint an imam for it, or you shall pray singly. He said: Mujahid said: That is not how he said it. Abu Mamar said: He said: Hudhayfa said to me: You shall appoint an imam for it, or you shall pray alone. Ibrahim said: It is the same — alone and singly are the same."
"1820","He said: My father informed me: he said: I heard some of the people of knowledge: that a people established the prayer, and this one kept saying to that one: Go forward, and that one saying to this one: Go forward, and they remained so until the earth swallowed them."
"1821","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me that he heard that when the imam falls short in the prayer, his sin and the sin of those behind him is upon him."
"1822","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: I said to Ata: Has it reached you that when the imam falls short in the prayer, the sin of those behind him is upon him? He said: Yes."
"1823","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: I said to Ata: Has it reached you that it was said: It is incumbent upon the imam not to supplicate for himself for anything but that he supplicate for those behind him the like of it? He said: Yes. I said: Then what is his right upon them? He said: They supplicate and seek forgiveness for themselves and for the believing men and women, and they do not single him out with anything except among the believers. I said: How does he supplicate? He said: He says: O Allah, forgive us, O Allah, have mercy on us, then he includes the believing men and women generally, beginning with them and singling them out, saying: O Allah, forgive us, O Allah, have mercy on us — this is special to them — then he includes the believing men and women generally afterwards, and he does not name those behind him except likewise."
"1824","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Bilal gives the adhan at night, so whoever intends fasting, Bilal's adhan should not prevent him, until he hears the adhan of Ibn Umm Maktum. Abu Bakr said: And one who heard Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrating from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, informed me the like of Mamar's hadith; from Malik, and Ibn Uyayna, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similarly."
"1825","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): that he said: Bilal gives the adhan at night, so eat and drink until you hear the call of Ibn Umm Maktum."
"1826","From Mamar, from Jafar ibn Burqan, from Shaddad, freedman of Iyad, from Thawban: he said: I gave the adhan once, then entered upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I have given the adhan, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Do not give the adhan until morning comes. Then I came to him again and said: I have given the adhan, and he said: Do not give the adhan until you see it like this — and he joined his hands then separated them."
"1827","From Mamar, from Ayyub: he said: Bilal gave the adhan once at night, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Go out and call out that the servant has slept. So he went out saying: Would that Bilal — may his mother lose him — were moistened from the sprinkling of the blood of his forehead. Then he called out that the servant slept."
"1828","From Yahya ibn al-Ala, from his uncle Shuayb ibn Khalid, from Zubayd al-Iyami, from Ibrahim al-Nakhai: he said: When the mu'adhdhin gave the adhan at night, they would come to him and say: Fear Allah, and repeat your adhan."
"1829","From Yahya ibn al-Ala, from al-Amash: he said: I think from Ibrahim: he said: They used to dislike that the mu'adhdhin give the adhan before the rising of dawn."
"1830","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: Sad ibn Ibrahim and others informed me that Ibn Umm Maktum and Bilal used to give the adhan for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Ibn Umm Maktum is blind, so when Ibn Umm Maktum gives the adhan, eat; and when Bilal gives the adhan, withhold, do not eat. Said said to me: And I do not think Bilal set out in the time of Umar to Sham."
"1831","From al-Thawri, from Ubaydillah, from Muhammad: he said: There was nothing between them except that this one descends and this one ascends."
"1832","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar: that he used to establish the iqama on journeys for every prayer, except the Fajr prayer, for he would give the adhan for it and establish it. From Abdillah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, similarly; from Mamar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, similarly; from Hisham ibn Hassan, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Ibn Umar, similarly."
"1833","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: I said to Nafi: How many adhans did Ibn Umar give on journeys? He said: Two adhans when dawn rose he gave the first; as for the rest of the prayers, iqama, iqama for each prayer. He used to say: Calling the adhan is only for an army or a traveling company with a leader over them, so he calls to prayer that they may gather for it; but as for a company like this, it is only the iqama."
"1834","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim: he said: An iqama suffices him on a journey."
"1835","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from Abu al-Aliya: he said: If you make the adhan the iqama, then it is from it."
"1836","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: I said to Ata: The caliph on a journey with him the like of pilgrims — how many adhans for him? He said: An adhan and an iqama for each prayer. I said: What about one who hears the iqama on a journey — is it incumbent upon him to come to prayer, as it is incumbent upon one who hears the call in residence to come to prayer? He said: Yes, unless he is upon his mount. I said: And if there is only toil and languor? He said: He laughed and said: Yes indeed, it is incumbent upon him to attend it."
"1837","From Abdillah ibn Umar, from Nafi: that Ibn Umar gave the adhan while at Dajnan between Mecca and Medina on an evening of wind and cold, and when he finished the call, he said to his companions: Pray in the saddles. Then he narrated that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to command that on a cold or rainy night, when he finished his adhan, saying: Pray in the saddles — twice."
"1838","From Ibn Uyayna, from Ayyub, from Nafi: that Ibn Umar gave the adhan at Dajnan between Mecca and Medina, and said: Pray in the saddles. Then he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to command his caller on a cold or rainy night, or one of wind, saying: Pray in the saddles."
"1839","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: Ata informed me that it reached him from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that rain caught him while on a journey, and he said to his companions: Pray in your saddles. I said to Ata: Do they pray with his prayer? He said: Yes, I think."
"1840","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: I said to Ata: One who hears the iqama on a journey and thinks he will catch it or part of it — is it incumbent upon him to come to it? And one who thinks he will not catch it — nothing incumbent upon him? I said: What about one who hears the iqama on the evening of Arafa — is it incumbent upon him to come to prayer when he hears it? He said: Yes, unless he is occupied with his mount."
"1841","From Mamar: he said: Ayyub used to give the adhan on journeys."
"1842","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: he said: Whoever is of the people of a village without a congregational mosque, they have an adhan and an iqama for each prayer. I said: Those dwelling at Arafa — how many for them? He said: An adhan and an iqama for each prayer; if they have an imam who gathers them, they have an adhan and an iqama for each prayer."
"1843","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: I said to Ata: A neighbor of mine in the desert — he established before me, or I established before him? He said: It is not incumbent upon either to come to his companion; you are the imam of your household, and he is the imam of his household."
"1844","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: I said to Ata: An imam of a people in the desert gives the adhan for Isha in his house and does not come out, does not appear to them. He said: Then they should not come to him. He said: For then he does not want them to come to him in his house."
"1845","From al-Thawri, from Zayd al-Ammi, from Abu Iyas, from Anas ibn Malik: he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Supplication between the adhan and the iqama is not rejected."
"1846","From Malik, from Abu Hazim, from Sahl ibn Sad al-Saidi: that he said: Two hours in which the gates of heaven are opened, and seldom is a supplicant's supplication rejected: at the presence of the call to prayer, and the rank in the path of Allah."
"1847","From Ayyub and Jabir al-Jufi: they said: Whoever says at the iqama: O Allah, Lord of this perfect call and the prayer to be established, grant our master Muhammad the Wasila and raise for him the ranks — intercession becomes due for him upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1848","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: Ata said: The first of the adhan is only to inform the people thereby. He said: So it is a binding duty, unavoidable, and it is not lawful for other than him, when he hears the adhan, but to come and witness the prayer. Then he informed me at that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: What is with men who hear the call to prayer then stay behind? I have resolved to establish the prayer, then no one stays behind from it but I burn his house — or burn upon him. He said: And a man came to him and said: O Prophet of Allah, I am blind, and it is hard upon me not to witness the prayer. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Witness it. He said: I am blind, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Do you hear the call? He said: Yes. He said: Then witness it. I said: What was his ailment? He said: I thought he was blind or of poor sight. And he asked leave regarding Isha. Ibn Jurayj said: And one whom I trust informed me that that man was Ibn Umm Maktum."
"1849","From Mamar, from Asim ibn Abi al-Najud, from Salih: he said: Ibn Umm Maktum came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), having suffered harm in his eyes, and said: Do you find a leave for me to pray in my house? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Do you hear the call? He said: Yes. He said: I find no leave for you. Mamar said: And I heard a man of the people of al-Jazira saying: Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do you hear success? He said: Yes. He said: Then answer."
"1850","From Ibn Jurayj and Ibrahim ibn Yazid: that Ali and Ibn Abbas said: Whoever hears the call and does not answer, he has no prayer. Ibn Abbas said: Except due to illness or excuse."
"1851","From al-Thawri and Ibn Uyayna, from Abu Hayyan, from his father, from Ali: he said: There is no prayer for the neighbor of the mosque except in the mosque. Al-Thawri said in his hadith: It was said to Ali: And who is the neighbor of the mosque? He said: Whoever hears the call."
"1852","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali: he said: Whoever hears the call among the neighbors of the mosque and does not answer, while healthy, without excuse, he has no prayer."
"1853","From Ibrahim ibn Tahman, from Mansur, from Adi ibn Thabit, from Aisha: she said: Whoever hears the call and does not answer, he did not intend good, nor was good intended for him."
"1854","From Ibn Uyayna, from Misar: that Aisha says: Whoever hears ""Come to prayer, come to success"" and does not answer, good is not increased for him thereby."
"1855","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: Ata said: No one of Allah's creation in residence or village has a leave to abandon it. I said: Even if he is over cloth of his which he sells, fearing if he rises from it it will be lost? He said: And he has no leave in that. I said: If he has inflammation of the eyes or a sickness not confining, or complains of his hands? He said: It is more beloved to me that he burden himself."
"1856","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: I said to Ata: What about one of the people of the village who does not hear the call? He said: If he wishes he comes, and if he wishes then not. He said: I said: Even if he is near the mosque? He said: If he wishes let him come, and if he wishes let him sit. I said: What if I am in a dwelling in which I sometimes hear it and sometimes do not — do I have a leave to sit when I do not hear it? He said: Yes. I said: Even if I know the prayer time has come, which I think it is prayed at? He said: Yes, when you do not hear the call."
"1857","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Said: that Umar ibn al-Khattab missed a man for days, then either he entered upon him or met him, saying: From where do you think? He said: I fell ill and did not go out to prayer nor to anything else. Umar said: If you answer anything, then answer success."
"1858","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: I said to Ata: One who hears the iqama in residence but did not hear the first? He said: If he thinks he will catch it, it is incumbent upon him to come to it."
"1859","Mamar informed us, from Asim ibn Sulayman, from Abdillah ibn al-Harith: that Ibn Abbas commanded his caller on a Friday in a rainy day, saying: When you reach ""Come to success,"" say: Pray in the saddles. It was said to him: What is this? He said: One better than me did it."
"1860","From al-Thawri, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abu Qilaba, from Abu al-Malih ibn Usama: he said: We prayed Isha at Basra and it rained upon us, then I came seeking to open, and my father Usama said to me: You saw us with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at the time of Hudaybiya, and it rained upon us though the sky did not wet the soles of our sandals, yet the caller of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called out: Pray in your saddles."
"1861","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me that Amr ibn Aws informed him that a man of Thaqif informed him that he heard the caller of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a rainy night saying: Come to prayer, come to success, pray in your saddles."
"1862","From Mamar, from Ubayd ibn Umayr, from a shaykh he named, from Nuaym ibn al-Nahham: he said: I heard the caller of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a cold night while I was in a blanket, and I wished he would say: Pray in your saddles. When he reached ""Come to success,"" he said: Pray in your saddles. Then I asked about it, and lo, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had commanded that."
"1863","From Ibn Jurayj, from Nafi, from Abdillah ibn Umar, from Nuaym ibn al-Nahham: he said: The caller of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave the adhan on a night in which there was cold, while I was under my blanket, and I wished that Allah would cast upon his tongue ""and no harm."" He said: And no harm."
"1864","From Mamar, from Zayd ibn Aslam: that Aisha said: Whoever hears the iqama then stands and prays, it is as if he prayed with the imam."
"1865","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Mahmud ibn al-Rabi, from Itban ibn Malik: he said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I have lost my sight, and the torrents come between me and the mosque of my people, and I wish you would come and pray in my house in a spot I take as a mosque. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I shall do so, Allah willing. He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by Abu Bakr and had him follow, so he went with him; he sought permission and entered, and said while standing: Where do you want me to pray? I pointed to him where I wanted. He said: Then we detained him over a khazira we had made for him; the people of the valley — meaning the people of the house — heard of him and flocked to him until the house filled. A man said: Where is Malik ibn al-Dukhshun, or Ibn al-Dukhaysh? A man said: That man is a hypocrite, loving neither Allah nor His Messenger. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not say it, while he says: There is no god but Allah, seeking thereby the Face of Allah. They said: O Messenger of Allah, as for us, we see his face and his talk among the hypocrites. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Also, do not say it, while he says: There is no god but Allah, seeking thereby the Face of Allah. They said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: No servant shall meet Allah on the Day of Resurrection saying: There is no god but Allah, seeking thereby the Face of Allah, but he is forbidden to the Fire. Mahmud said: I narrated this hadith to a group among whom was Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, and he said: I do not think the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said what you said. He said: I swore if I returned to Itban ibn Malik I would ask him; I returned to him and found him an old shaykh whose sight had gone, leading his people, and I sat beside him and asked him about this hadith, and he narrated it to me as he had narrated it to me the first time. Mamar said: Al-Zuhri, when he narrated this hadith, would say: Afterwards obligations and matters came down, and we think the matter ended at them; so whoever can avoid being deceived, let him not be deceived."
"1866","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwa: he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), after the mu'adhdhin established the prayer and they fell silent, would speak of needs and fulfill them; so a staff was made for him in the qibla like a peg he would hold onto for that."
"1867","Mamar informed us, from Thabit, from Anas: he said: The prayer would be established, and a man would speak to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about a need of his, standing between him and the qibla, and he would remain standing speaking to him; sometimes I saw some of the people dozing from the length of the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) standing."
"1868","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abdillah ibn Abi Qatada, from his father Abu Qatada al-Ansari: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the prayer is established, do not stand until you see me."
"1869","From al-Thawri, from Fitr, from Abu Khalid al-Walibi: that Ali came out to them when the prayer was established while they were standing, and said: What is with you, idly gazing?"
"1870","From al-Thawri, from Zubayr ibn Adi, from Ibrahim: he said: I asked him: Standing or sitting do you await the imam? He said: Rather sitting."
"1871","From Mamar: he said: I came to Abu Ishaq — and he was a neighbor of the mosque who would not go out until he heard the iqama. He said: And I saw men doing that."
"1872","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: I said to Ata: It is said, when the mu'adhdhin says: The prayer has begun, the people should stand then? He said: Yes."
"1873","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: Abdillah ibn Abi Yazid informed me, from Husayn ibn Ali ibn Abi Talib: he said — and I saw him in the Zamzam basin from which the pilgrims are given drink, the basin then being between the Corner and Zamzam — the mu'adhdhin established the prayer, and when he said: The prayer has begun, Husayn stood, that being after the death of Muawiya, while the people of Mecca had no imam. It was said to him: Sit until the people form rows, for he says: The prayer has begun."
"1874","From Ibn Uyayna: he said: Ubaydillah ibn Abi Yazid informed me: he said: I saw Husayn ibn Ali wading in Zamzam, and a dispute arose between Ibn al-Zubayr and a man at the establishment of prayer, and I saw Husayn standing in the basin. It was said to him: Sit. And he says: The prayer has begun — twice."
"1875","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: Abd al-Karim ibn Malik informed me that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz sent men to the mosque: When the prayer is established, stand to it."
"1876","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Muhammad ibn Ubaydillah, from Atiyya: he said: We were sitting with Ibn Umar, and when the mu'adhdhin began the iqama we stood, and Ibn Umar said: Sit; when he says: The prayer has begun, then stand."
"1877","From Dawud ibn Qays, from Zura ibn Ibrahim: that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz used to charge the guards, when the mu'adhdhin began the iqama, to make the people stand to prayer until he says the takbir."
"1878","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: Ibn Shihab informed me that the people, at the hour the mu'adhdhin says: Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest, establishing the prayer, would stand to prayer, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not come to his place until the rows were straightened."
"1879","From Ibn Uyayna, from Amr ibn Ubayd: he said: I asked al-Hasan: he said: I said: We pass by the mosque and hear the iqama, wanting to pass it to another. He said: A man among the Muslims would say to his brother when he heard the iqama: You have been detained."
"1880","From Hisham, from al-Hasan: he said: When a man hears the adhan, he has been detained."
"1881","From Mamar, from Ibrahim ibn Uqba: he said: A man came to Ibn al-Musayyib while he was in the mosque and asked him about a need of his, then went to leave. Ibn al-Musayyib said: Where do you intend? He said: My companions await me. Ibn al-Musayyib said: The adhan has been given, so do not leave. He said: They are upon their mounts, and I dislike detaining them. Ibn al-Musayyib said: Do not leave until you pray. He said: Ibn al-Musayyib was distracted from him, and the man slipped away and left. Ibn al-Musayyib turned and said: Where is the man? They said: He left. He said: I do not think he will attain any good in this journey of his. He had gone but a few miles when he fell from his mount, his riding camel, and his leg broke."
"1882","From Ibn Uyayna: he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Harmala narrated to me: he said: I was with Ibn al-Musayyib when a man came to him and asked him about some matter, and the caller called, and he wanted to leave. Said said to him: Prayer has been called. The man said: My companions have gone, and this is my mount at the door. He said: He said to him: Do not leave, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: None leaves the mosque after the call except a hypocrite, except a man who leaves for a need while intending to return to prayer. But the man refused but to leave. Said said: There is the man. For I was with him one day when a man came to him and said: O Abu Muhammad, did you not see this man — he refused but to leave — he fell from his mount and his leg broke. Said said to him: I thought something would befall him."
"1883","From al-Thawri, from Ibrahim ibn Muhajir, from Abu al-Shatha: he said: We were with Abu Hurayra in the mosque, and the caller called to Asr, and a man left. Abu Hurayra said: As for this one, he has disobeyed Abu al-Qasim."
"1884","From al-Thawri, from Mughira: he said: When you hear the iqama, do not leave the mosque. And Ibrahim was: In the adhan, more strict than in the iqama."
"1885","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Khuthaym, from Mujahid: he said: I came, I and Ibn Umar, while the people were in prayer, and we sat by the gardens until they finished."
"1886","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damrah, from Ali: he said: Any man who goes out in a barren land, meaning a wasteland, let him choose well for the prayer, and let him cast his glance to the right and to the left, and let him look for the easiest foothold and the most pleasant place for his prayer spot, for the tracts of land compete for the Muslim man; every spot loves that Allah be remembered in it. If he wishes he gives the adhan, and if he wishes he (only) gives the iqamah."
"1887","From Ma'mar, from Mansur, from a man, from Abdullah ibn Umar: he said: When a man is in a deserted land and he gives the adhan and the iqamah and prays, four thousand angels pray with him, or four thousand thousand angels."
"1888","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father: he said: When a man prays and gives the iqamah, his two angels pray with him, and when he gives the adhan and the iqamah, many angels pray with him."
"1889","From Ibn Jurayj, from Makhul: he said: When a man gives the iqamah for himself, his two angels pray with him, and when he gives the adhan and the iqamah, as many angels as fill the earth pray with him."
"1890","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib: he said: Whoever prays in a desert land and gives the iqamah, an angel prays on his right and an angel on his left, and whoever gives the adhan and the iqamah, angels like mountains in number pray with him."
"1891","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, from Salman al-Farisi: he said: The Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When a man is in a barren land and the prayer becomes due, let him perform ablution, and if he finds no water let him perform tayammum. If he gives the iqamah, his two angels pray with him, and if he gives the adhan and the iqamah, troops of Allah pray behind him whose two ends cannot be seen."
"1892","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: I said to Ata: I forgot the two rak'ahs of Fajr until the prayer was commenced. He said: Pray them, then pray (with the congregation), and do not repeat the iqamah; the first one suffices you."
"1893","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: he said: Every prayer has an iqamah without fail, even if you pray by yourself, and even if you are on a journey."
"1894","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: I said to Ata: I prayed the obligatory prayer by myself and forgot to give the iqamah for it. He said: Go back to your prayer: give the iqamah for it, then repeat it."
"1895","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri and Qatada: they said: Whoever forgets the iqamah until he has prayed, he does not repeat his prayer."
"1896","From al-Thawri, from Mansur: he said: I said to Ibrahim: I prayed without iqamah. He said: It suffices you."
"1897","From Abu Hanifah, from Hammad, from Ibrahim: that Ibn Mas'ud prayed with his companions in his house without iqamah, and said: The iqamah of the city suffices."
"1898","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim: that Ibn Mas'ud, Uthman and al-Aswad prayed without adhan or iqamah. Sufyan said: The iqamah of the city sufficed them."
"1899","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, concerning a man who forgot the iqamah until he stood praying: he said: When Ibn Umar was in a city in which the prayer was established, that sufficed him."
"1900","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim: he said: When you are in the city, the iqamah of the city suffices you, even if you do not hear it."
"1901","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ikrimah ibn Khalid, from Abdullah ibn Waqid: he said: When Ibn Umar prayed in a land where the prayer was established, he would pray with their iqamah and would not give iqamah for himself."
"1902","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Abi Ziyad: he said: I asked Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, and said: I came to the mosque and they had already prayed; shall I give the iqamah? He said: You have been spared (it suffices you)."
"1903","From Ibn Jurayj, from Sulayman, from Abu Uthman: he said: I saw Anas enter a mosque in which prayer had already been offered, and he gave the adhan and the iqamah."
"1904","From al-Thawri, from Yunus, from al-Hasan: he said: There is no iqamah upon women. He said: Whoever forgets the iqamah on a journey, there is no repetition upon him, and whoever forgets rinsing the mouth and nose (in ablution) does not repeat."
"1905","From al-Thawri, from Mansur: he said: I said to Ibrahim: I forgot the iqamah on a journey. He said: Your prayer suffices you."
"1906","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: If you are on a journey, do not pray except with the iqamah, but if you forget the iqamah, go back to your prayer, give the iqamah, then repeat it."
"1907","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: I said to Ata: What if one hears the call or the iqamah while he is praying the obligatory prayer - does he break his prayer and come to the congregational mosque? He said: If he thinks he will catch something of the obligatory prayer, yes. I said: What if I hear the iqamah - is it binding on me to come to the prayer as it is binding when I hear the call? He said: Yes."
"1908","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi: that Ibn Umar prayed two rak'ahs of the obligatory prayer in his house, then heard the iqamah and went out to it."
"1909","From al-Thawri, from al-Rabi' ibn Abi Rashid: he said: I saw Sa'id ibn Jubayr come to us when we had already prayed, and he heard a muezzin and went out to him."
"1910","From al-Thawri, from al-Hasan ibn Ubaydillah, from Ibrahim: he said: Al-Aswad did it; he would say: Once I follow the mosque (congregation)."
"1911","From al-Thawri, from al-Hasan ibn Umar, from Fudayl, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah: that he would come to the mosque when they had already prayed in it, while he heard the muezzins, and he would pray in the mosque he had entered."
"1912","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, concerning a man who prayed one rak'ah of the obligatory prayer then heard the iqamah: he said: He joins another to it, then comes to the imam and prays with him in congregation; and if he is in the mosque, he joins them."
"1913","From Ma'mar, from Abd al-Karim, from Ziyad ibn Abi Maryam, from Ibn Mas'ud: he said: When the prayer has been commenced, do not leave it for another."
"1914","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, that he was asked about a man who gave the adhan then forgot and gave the iqamah. Al-Sha'bi said: He gives the adhan and gives the iqamah. He said: Its interpretation in our view is that he makes the iqamah serve as the adhan, then gives the iqamah."
"1915","From al-Thawri, from Ibrahim ibn Muslim, from Abu al-Ahwas: he said: Abdullah said: Whoever wishes to meet Allah tomorrow as a Muslim, let him guard these prescribed prayers where they are called for, for they are among the ways of guidance, and Allah has legislated for your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the ways of guidance. By my life, I do not think any of you but has taken a mosque in his house; yet if you prayed in your houses as this absentee prays in his house, you would abandon the way of your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and if you abandoned the way of your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), you would go astray. We have seen ourselves, and none stayed away from it except a hypocrite whose hypocrisy was known, or whose hypocrisy was evident. I have seen a man supported between two men until he was stood in the row. There is no man who purifies himself and performs the purification well, then takes a step heading thereby to a mosque of Allah the Exalted, except Allah writes for him thereby a good deed, raises him thereby a degree, and removes from him thereby a sin, so much so that we would shorten our steps. From Ma'mar, from Layth, raising it to Ibn Mas'ud, the like of it."
"1916","From Malik, from Nu'aym ibn Muhammad, the freedman of Umar, from Abu Hurayrah: he said: The furthest of you in house has the greatest reward. They said: How, O Abu Hurayrah? He said: The multitude of steps: Allah writes for him for one of his two steps a good deed, and erases from him for the other a sin."
"1917","From al-Thawri, from Tarif, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Sa'id: he said: Banu Salamah complained to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the distance of their dwellings from the mosque, so Allah revealed: And We record what they have sent forth and their traces. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Keep to your dwellings, for your traces are recorded."
"1918","From Ja'far ibn Sulayman, from Thabit, from Anas: he said: Zayd ibn Thabit placed his hand on me while he was heading for the prayer, and he kept shortening his steps."
"1919","From Ma'mar, from Hammam ibn Munabbih, from Abu Hurayrah: he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, I have intended to command my youths to prepare bundles of firewood for me, then command a man to lead the people in prayer, then burn houses down over those in them."
"1920","From Abdullah ibn Muharrar, from Yazid ibn al-Asamm, from Abu Hurayrah: he said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: I have intended to command my youths to gather bundles of firewood for me, then command a man to lead the people in prayer, then go and burn the houses of a people who do not attend the prayer. From Ma'mar, from Ja'far ibn Burqan, from Yazid ibn al-Asamm, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"1921","From Ma'mar, from al-A'mash, from Abu Salih or another, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to this: he said: If it were said to one of you: if you attend Isha you will find two good game-cuts, or a fat meat-strip, he would attend it. And no prayer is harder on the hypocrites than these two prayers: the dawn prayer and the night prayer; they cannot bear them."
"1922","From Ma'mar, from Ja'far ibn Burqan, from Thabit ibn al-Hajjaj: he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab went out to the prayer and met the people, so he ordered the muezzin and he gave the iqamah, and said: We do not wait for anyone for our prayer. When he finished his prayer he turned to the people, then said: What is the matter with people whose lagging holds back others? By Allah, I have intended to send to them so they are brought by their necks, then it is said: Attend the prayer."
"1923","From Ma'mar, from Layth, from Mujahid: he said: I witnessed a man who stayed with Ibn Abbas for a month, asking him about this question every day: What do you say about a man who fasts by day and stands (in prayer) by night, but does not attend congregation or Friday - where is he? He said: In the Fire."
"1924","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Mujahid: he said: A man asked Ibn Abbas and said: A man who fasts by day and stands (in prayer) by night, but does not attend congregation or Friday - where is he? He said: In the Fire. Then he came the next day and asked him about that, and he said: He is in the Fire. He kept coming to him for nearly a month asking him about that, while Ibn Abbas kept saying: He is in the Fire."
"1925","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri: he said: Urwah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to me, from Ubaydillah ibn al-Adi ibn al-Khiyar, that he entered upon Uthman ibn Affan while he was besieged, while Ali was leading the people in prayer, and said: O Commander of the Believers! I feel uneasy praying with these people while you are the imam. Uthman said: Prayer is the best of what people do; so when you see people doing good, do good with them, and when you see them doing evil, avoid their evil."
"1926","From Ma'mar, from a man, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Thawban, from his grandfather: he said: There is no step a Muslim takes toward a mosque except Allah writes for him thereby a good deed and erases from him thereby a sin."
"1927","From Malik, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah: he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Shall I not guide you to what Allah expiates sins with and raises ranks with: steps to the mosques, performing ablution fully despite hardships, and waiting for the prayer after the prayer - that is the ribat, that is the ribat."
"1928","From Muhammad ibn Abi Humayd: he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id al-Maqburi informed me, from Abu Hurayrah: he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Ribat - the best ribat is prayer after prayer, and keeping to the gatherings of remembrance. There is no servant who prays then sits in his place except the angels invoke blessings upon him until he breaks his ablution."
"1929","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Abu Ma'shar, from Ibrahim: he said: They used to hope for forgiveness for a man when he walked to the mosque, meaning for the prayer on a dark night."
"1930","From Ma'mar, from Zayd ibn Aslam: that Ka'b said: Whoever goes to the mosque morning and evening, Allah makes the heaven and the earth - or he said the heavens - guarantee his provision. Abd al-Razzaq doubts."
"1931","From Ma'mar, from Aban, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Ata: he said: Satan is the wolf of the son of Adam, like the wolf of sheep: it seizes the sheep far from the side and the outlying one. So keep to the congregation and the mosques."
"1932","From Ibn Abi Sabrah, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah: he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I have intended to command youths to gather firewood, then command a man to lead the people in prayer, then come to the houses of people who did not attend the prayer and burn them over them. By Allah, if it were said to one of them: if he comes to the mosque he will find one game-cut or two, or one meat-strip or two, he would attend it."
"1933","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: Ata said: The excellence of prayer in congregation is twenty-five fold."
"1934","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: Umar ibn Ata ibn Abi al-Khuwar informed me, that while he was sitting with Nafi' ibn Jubayr, Abu Abdullah the in-law of Zayd ibn al-Zayyan passed by, so Nafi' called him and said: I heard Abu Hurayrah say: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A prayer with the imam is better than twenty-five prayers one prays alone."
"1935","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah: he said: I heard Abu Hurayrah say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The excellence of the congregational prayer over the prayer of one alone is twenty-five degrees, and the angels of the night and the angels of the day gather at the dawn prayer. Abu Hurayrah says: And recite if you wish: And the recitation of dawn; indeed the recitation of dawn is ever witnessed. Ma'mar said: Qatada said: The angels of the night and the angels of the day attend it."
"1936","From Ma'mar, from one who heard al-Hasan say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A man's prayer in congregation exceeds a man's prayer alone by twenty-four prayers."
"1937","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Ahwas, from Ibn Mas'ud: he said: The excellence of congregational prayer over a man's prayer alone is twenty-odd degrees."
"1938","Al-Thawri informed us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abdullah ibn Abi al-Basir, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b: he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led us in the Fajr prayer, and when he gave the salam he said: Is so-and-so present? They said: Yes, but he did not attend. He said it three times. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The heaviest prayers upon the hypocrites are the night prayer and the dawn prayer; if they knew what is in them they would come to them even crawling. The first row is like the row of the angels; if you knew its merit you would hasten to it. Your prayer with one man is purer than your prayer alone, your prayer with two men is purer than your prayer with one man, and the more the more beloved to Allah."
"1939","From Ubaydillah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar: he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The excellence of a man's prayer in congregation over a man's prayer alone is twenty-five degrees. He informed us, he said: Ma'mar informed us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abdullah ibn Ubayy, from Abdullah ibn Abi Basir, the first report."
"1940","From Malik, from Sumayy, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah: he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If people knew what is in the call and the first row, then found no way but to draw lots for it, they would draw lots. If they knew what is in coming early, they would race to it. If they knew what is in attending the night prayer and the dawn, they would come to them crawling. He said: I said to Malik: What is disliked in saying: al-Atamah? He said: Thus said the one who narrated to me."
"1941","From al-Thawri, from Uthman ibn Hakim, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Amrah, from Uthman ibn Affan: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever prays the night prayer in congregation, it is like standing half the night, and whoever prays the night prayer and the dawn in congregation, it is like standing the (whole) night."
"1942","From Ibn Jurayj, from Yahya ibn Sa'id: he said: Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Harith al-Taymi informed me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Amrah al-Ansari: he said: Uthman went out to the last night prayer and found the people few, so he lay down a little at the rear of the mosque until the people increased. He said: So I lay down, and he asked me: Who are you? So I informed him. Then he asked me: What do you have of the Qur'an with you? So I informed him. Then Uthman said: Indeed whoever attends the night prayer, it is as if he stood half the night, and whoever attends the dawn, it is as if he stood the (whole) night."
"1943","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: Ibn Abi Mulaykah said: Shifa, one of the women of Banu Adi ibn Ka'b, came to Umar in Ramadan, and he said: Why do I not see Abu Hathmah, her husband - he is one of the men of Banu Adi ibn Ka'b - attend the dawn? She said: O Commander of the Believers! He toiled through his night, then was too weary to go out, so he prayed the dawn then slept? He said: By Allah, had he attended it, it would have been more beloved to me than the toil of his night."
"1944","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Sulayman ibn Abi Hathmah, from al-Shifa bint Abdillah: she said: Umar ibn al-Khattab entered my house and found with me two sleeping men, and said: What is the matter with these two that they did not attend the prayer with me? I said: O Commander of the Believers! They prayed with the people, and that was in Ramadan, and they kept praying until morning, then prayed the dawn and slept. Umar said: For me to pray the dawn in congregation is more beloved to me than to pray the night until morning."
"1945","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abu Sulaym, the freedman of Umm Ali, from Mujahid: he said: The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to a man of the Ansar: Attending them both - the night prayer and the dawn - is better than standing (in prayer) between them."
"1946","From Ma'mar, from Qatada: that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: For me to pray the night prayer in congregation is more beloved to me than to keep alive the whole night."
"1947","From Yahya ibn Abi Kathir: he said: The dawn prayer in congregation used to equal standing the whole night, and the night prayer half the night."
"1948","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: Ata said: Attending a prescribed prayer, whichever it is, is more beloved to me than standing a night and fasting a day."
"1949","From Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Rawwad, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar: he said: When he attended the last night prayer with the people, he would pray some rak'ahs then sleep, and when he did not attend it in congregation, he would keep alive the night. He said: One of Ma'mar's people informed me that he used to do it, so I narrated it to Ma'mar. He said: Ayyub used to do it."
"1950","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, from Ibn al-Musayyib: he said: Whoever prays Maghrib and the night prayer in congregation does not miss the good of Laylat al-Qadr."
"1951","From Ma'mar, from Asim ibn Sulayman, from Abu al-Aliyah: he said: I do not know whether he raised it: he said: Whoever attends the five prayers for forty nights in congregation, catching the first takbir, Paradise becomes due for him."
"1952","Al-Thawri narrated to us, from Asim al-Ahwal, from Asim, from Anas: he said: Whoever does not miss the first rak'ah of the prayer for forty days, two declarations of innocence are written for him: innocence from the Fire and innocence from hypocrisy."
"1953","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir: that a man was negligent or lagged behind the prayer until the imam said the takbir. Ibn Mas'ud and Ibn Umar said: What you missed of it is better than a thousand."
"1954","Isra'il informed us, from Abu Yahya, from Mujahid: he said: I heard a man from the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - I know him only as one who attended Badr - say to his son: Did you catch the prayer with us? He said: Yes. He said: Did you catch the first takbir? He said: No. He said: What you missed of it is better than a hundred she-camels, all black-eyed."
"1955","From Isra'il, from Abu Sinan, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr: I heard him say: For me to pray with an imam who recites: Has there reached you the story of the Overwhelming, is more beloved to me than to recite a hundred verses in my prayer."
"1956","From Hushaym ibn Bashir, from Abu Bishr Ja'far ibn Abi Wahshiyah Wahshiyah: Abu Umayr ibn Anas said: Uncles of mine of the Ansar, from the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), narrated to me, saying: No hypocrite attended them both - meaning the dawn and the night prayer."
"1957","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, and Hisham, from al-Hasan, and Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri and Qatada: they said: Three are a congregation."
"1958","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Kathir ibn Aflah: he said: Zayd ibn Thabit entered upon us in the treasury and led us in the Asr prayer, then said: The prayer of the congregation exceeds a man's prayer alone by twenty-odd."
"1959","From Isra'il ibn Yunus, from Simak ibn Harb: he said: I heard Ibn Samurah say: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed the morning prayer, he would sit in his place until the sun rose."
"1960","Muhammad ibn Abi Humayd narrated to us: he said: Hazim ibn Tammam informed me, from Abbas ibn Sahl al-Ansari then al-Sa'idi - thus he said - from his father or his grandfather: he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: For me to pray the dawn then sit in my place remembering Allah until the sun rises is more beloved to me than charging on swift horses in the path of Allah."
"1961","Muhammad ibn Abi Humayd said, and our shaykhs narrated to us, that Ali ibn Abi Talib said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: For me to pray the dawn and sit remembering Allah until the sun rises is more beloved to me than all over which the sun rises and sets."
"1962","From al-Thawri and Ibn Abi Sabrah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith: he said: Hakim ibn Hakim narrated to me, from Nafi' ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas: he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Gabriel led me at the House: he prayed Zuhr with me when the sun declined, when it was the measure of a sandal-strap, then prayed Asr with me when the shadow of everything was its like, then prayed Maghrib with me when the fasting person breaks fast, then prayed the night prayer with me when the twilight disappeared, then prayed Fajr with me when food and drink become forbidden to the fasting person. He said: Then he prayed with me the next day Zuhr when the shadow of everything was its like, then prayed with me Asr when the shadow of everything was twice its like, then prayed with me Maghrib when the fasting person breaks fast, then prayed with me the night prayer in the first third of the night, then prayed with me Fajr at isfar, then turned to me and said: O Muhammad! This is the time of the prophets before you; the time is between these two times."
"1963","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Umar ibn Nafi, from Jubayr ibn Mut'im, and from his father, from Ibn Abbas: he said: Gabriel came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when the sun declined, and said to him: Stand and pray. So he prayed Zuhr. Then he came to him when the shadow of everything was its like, and said: Stand and pray. So he prayed Asr. Then he came to him when the sun set and night entered, and said: Stand and pray. So he prayed Maghrib. Then he came to him when the twilight disappeared, and said to him: Stand and pray. So he prayed the night prayer. Then he came to him when the dawn shone, and said: Stand and pray the dawn. Then he came to him the next day when the shadow of everything was twice its like, and said to him: Stand and pray. So he prayed Asr. Then he came to him when the sun set and night entered, and said: Stand and pray. So he prayed Maghrib. Then he came to him when a third of the night had gone, and said: Stand and pray. So he prayed the night prayer. Then he came when it was isfar, and said to him: Stand and pray. So he prayed the dawn. Then he said to him: This is the prayer of the prophets before you, so hold to it."
"1964","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: Nafi' ibn Jubayr and others said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) woke in the morning after the night in which he was taken by night, nothing startled him but Gabriel. He came down when the sun declined, and for that it was called the first. He stood and called his Companions: Prayer in congregation. So they gathered. Gabriel prayed with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed with the people: he lengthened the first two rak'ahs, then shortened the remaining two. Then Gabriel gave salam to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the people. Then he came down at Asr likewise, and they did as they had done at Zuhr. Then he came down at the beginning of the night, and called: Prayer in congregation. Gabriel prayed for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for the people: he lengthened in the first two and shortened in the third. Then Gabriel gave salam to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the people. Then when a third of the night had gone, he came down and called the people: Prayer in congregation. They gathered. Gabriel prayed for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for the people: he recited in the first two, lengthened and recited aloud, and shortened in the remaining two. Then Gabriel gave salam to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the people. Then when the dawn rose, Gabriel prayed the dawn with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the people: he recited in them, aloud and at length, and raised his voice. Then Gabriel gave salam to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the people."
"1965","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: I said to Ata: The times of prayer? He said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: The times of prayer? He said: Attend the prayer with me today and tomorrow. He prayed Zuhr when the sun declined. He said: Then he prayed Asr, hastening it. Then he prayed Maghrib when night entered, when the fasting person breaks fast. As for the night prayer, I do not know when he prayed it - another besides Ata said: until the twilight disappeared. Ata said: Then he prayed the dawn when the dawn rose. Then he prayed Zuhr the next day, delaying it until it was cool. I said: The first cooling? He said: After and after, toward evening. He said: Then he prayed Asr after that, delaying it. I said: What delay? He said: Toward evening, before the sun takes on yellowness. He said: Then he prayed Maghrib when the twilight disappeared. He said: He said: I do not know at what time he prayed the night prayer - another said: he prayed at a third of the night. Ata said: Then he prayed the dawn at isfar, very bright. I said: At what time? He said: Before its time lapses, before the time the sun rises comes. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Where is the one who asked me about the time of prayer? So he was brought, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Did you attend the prayer with me today and yesterday? He said: Then pray it between that. He said: Then Ali turned, and said: I think he used to pray it between that - meaning the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1966","From Ma'mar, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm, from his father: that Gabriel came down and prayed the Zuhr prayer with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed the prayer when the sun declined. Then he prayed Asr when the shadow of everything was its like. Then he prayed Maghrib when the sun set. Then he prayed the night prayer after that, as if meaning the disappearance of the twilight. Then he prayed the dawn in the darkness, when the dawn broke. He said: Then Gabriel came down the next day and prayed with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed with the people Zuhr when the shadow of everything was its like. Then he prayed Asr when the shadow of everything was twice its like. Then he prayed Maghrib when the sun set, at one time. Then he prayed the night prayer after a portion of the night had gone. Then he prayed the dawn after it was very bright. Then he said: Between these two times is time."
"1967","From al-Thawri, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, from his father, and from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad: he said: Gabriel came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and prayed Zuhr with him when the sun declined."
"1968","Ma'mar, from Qatada, from Ibn Mas'ud: he said: Prayer has a time like the time of Hajj; so pray the prayer at its time."
"1969","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, from Abu al-Aliyah al-Riyahi: that Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote to Abu Musa: Pray Zuhr when the sun declines from the middle of the heaven, pray Asr when the sun slopes while it is white and clear, pray Maghrib when the sun sets, pray the night prayer when the twilight disappears, until whenever you wish - it used to be said: to half the night is attainment, and what is after that is excess - and pray the dawn while the stars are visible and intermingled, and lengthen the recitation. Know that combining two prayers without excuse is among the major sins."
"1970","From Malik, from his uncle Abu Suhayl ibn Malik, from his father: that Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote to Abu Musa al-Ash'ari: Pray Zuhr when the sun declines, Asr while the sun is white and clear before yellowness enters it, Maghrib when the sun sets, delay the night prayer so long as you do not sleep, and pray the dawn while the stars are visible and intermingled, and recite in it two long surahs of the Mufassal."
"1971","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar: he said: Umar wrote to the people of the garrison towns: Pray Zuhr when the sun declines until the shadow of everything is its like, Asr while the sun remains, as long as a rider travels two or three farsakhs, Maghrib when the sun sets and night enters, the night prayer when the twilight disappears until a third of the night. Do not be distracted from the prayer; whoever sleeps - may his eye not sleep, whoever sleeps - may his eye not sleep."
"1972","From Malik, from Nafi: that Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote to his governors: The most important of your affairs with me is the prayer; whoever guards it and maintains it guards his religion, and whoever wastes it is more wasting of others. Then he wrote: Pray Zuhr when the shade is an arm-length until the shadow of one of you is its like, Asr while the sun is high, white and clear, as long as a rider travels two or three farsakhs, Maghrib when the sun sets, the night prayer when the twilight disappears until a third of the night - whoever sleeps, may his eye not sleep; whoever sleeps, may his eye not sleep - and the dawn while the stars are visible and intermingled. From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, the like of it."
"1973","Ma'mar informed us, from Abdullah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym, from Ibn Labibah: he said: I came to Abu Hurayrah while he was sitting in the Sacred Mosque. He said: I said: Describe him to me. He said: He was a dark man with two braids, wide between the shoulders, hollow of the two front teeth. I said: Inform me of the chief of matters - he meant - about our prayer which we must have. He said: Who are you? He said: From a people pleased with their obedience, enveloped in it. He said: From whom are you? I said: From Thaqif. He said: Where are you with respect to Amr ibn Aws? He said: I said: I saw it was Amr, but I came to ask you. He said: Do you recite anything of the Qur'an? I said: Yes. He said: So I recited for him the Opening of the Book. He said: These are the seven oft-repeated which Allah the Exalted mentions: And We have given you seven of the oft-repeated and the Qur'an. He said: Then he said to me: Do you recite the surah of al-Ma'idah? I said: Yes. He said: Recite for me the verse of ablution. So I recited it. He said: I see you know the ablution of prayer. Did you not hear Allah say: Establish the prayer at the decline of the sun - do you know what the decline of the sun is? I said: No. He said: When the sun declines from the middle of the heaven, or from the belly of the heaven after midday. He said: Yes. So pray Zuhr then, and pray Asr while the sun is white and clear, you find for it a touch (of heat). He said: Do you know what the darkness of the night is? He said: I said: Yes, the setting of the sun. He said: Yes. Hasten it right after it, then hasten it right after it. Pray the night prayer when the twilight is gone and night darkens from here - and he pointed to the east - between you and a third of the night. The sooner after the disappearance of the whiteness of the horizon, the better. Pray the dawn when the dawn rises - do you know the dawn? He said: I said: Yes. He said: Not all people know it. He said: I said: When it spreads with whiteness. He said: Yes. Pray it then, up to the gloom, then up to the gloom. He said in his report: Beware of squatting (in prayer) and guard against forgetfulness until you finish. He said: I said: Inform me of the middle prayer. He said: Did you not hear Allah say: Establish the prayer at the decline of the sun to the darkness of the night, and the recitation of dawn - the verse - and: after the night prayer, three times of privacy for you. So he mentioned all the prayers, then said: Guard the prayers and the middle prayer - indeed it is Asr, indeed it is Asr."
"1974","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash: he said: The companions of Abdullah ibn Mas'ud would hasten Zuhr, delay Asr, hasten Maghrib, and delay the night prayer."
"1975","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri: he said: We were with Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz when he delayed the Asr prayer once, so Urwah said to him: Bashir ibn Abi Mas'ud al-Ansari narrated to me that al-Mughirah delayed the prayer once - meaning Asr - so Abu Mas'ud said to him: By Allah! O Mughirah! You know that Gabriel came down and prayed, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed, then the people prayed with him, then he came down and prayed, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed, until he counted five prayers. Umar said to him: Look at what you say, O Urwah - did Gabriel prescribe the time of prayer? Urwah said to him: Thus Bashir ibn Abi Mas'ud narrated to me. He said: He kept teaching the time of prayer by a sign until he left the world."
"1976","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: Ibn Shihab narrated to me that he heard Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz asking Urwah. Urwah ibn al-Zubayr said: Al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah made Asr prayer late while he was over Kufa, so Abu Mas'ud al-Ansari entered upon him and said to him: O Mughirah! You know - Gabriel came down and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed, and the people prayed five times at his word, he says. Then he said: Thus I was commanded. Umar said to Urwah: Know what you say - did Gabriel establish the time of prayer? Urwah said: Thus Bashir ibn Abi Mas'ud used to narrate, from his father."
"1977","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri: he said: Anas ibn Malik informed me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed Zuhr when the sun declined."
"1978","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: I said to Ata: What time is most beloved to you for me to pray Zuhr, as imam and alone? He said: When it cools, or after the cooling, and do not make evening of it. I said: What about winter? He said: Also when it cools, and before the time you pray it in summer, because of the cold. I said: What if I pray it in a house in shade? He said: Also when it cools is most beloved to me."
"1979","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: he said: I heard Abu Hurayrah say: Cool (delay) the prayer, for the severity of heat is from the blast of Hell."
"1980","From Ibn Jurayj and Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah: he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the heat is severe, cool the prayer, for the severity of heat is from the blast of Hell."
"1981","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin: he said: It reached me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Cool Zuhr, for the severity of heat is from the blast of Hell. One of them said: from the blast of Hell. From Ma'mar, from Hammam ibn Munabbih, from Abu Hurayrah: he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said the like of it."
"1982","From al-Thawri, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar: he said: The decline of the sun is its passing the meridian after midday, and that is the time of Zuhr."
"1983","From Ibn Jurayj, from Sulayman ibn Musa: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Zuhr prayer is when the sun inclines."
"1984","He said: Abdullah ibn Umar used to say: We used to pray Zuhr with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when the sun inclined from a man's shadow by an arm or two."
"1985","Ibn Jurayj said: The closest Zuhr was to the sun's passing the meridian was most beloved to Tawus, and he used to say: The sooner I pray it the more beloved to me - except that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered cooling Zuhr in the heat. Ibn Tawus mentioned it, from his father."
"1986","From al-Thawri, from Hakim ibn Jubayr, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from A'ishah: she said: I never saw anyone hasten Zuhr more than the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: She did not except her father or Umar."
"1987","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Sa'id ibn Wahb, from Khubab: he said: We complained to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the hot ground, but he did not relieve us - he says: concerning the Zuhr prayer."
"1988","From al-Thawri: he said: Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil narrated to me, from Jabir: he said: Zuhr is like its name, he says: at midday."
"1989","From Ma'mar, from Aban, from Anas: he said: We used to pray Zuhr in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in winter, and we did not know whether more of the day had passed or what remained."
"1990","From Ma'mar, from Budayl al-Uqayli, from Abu al-Ala ibn Abdullah ibn al-Shikhkhir, from a woman he named: she said: I used to pray Zuhr with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I would recognize its time in the heaven and the earth, by the sun - he used to pray it when the sun declined."
"1991","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi: he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab used to pray Zuhr when the sun declined."
"1992","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub and Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Ikrimah ibn Khalid: he said: Umar came to Makkah, and Abu Mahdhurah gave the call for him. He said to him: Did you not fear your thin flank would burst? He said: O Commander of the Believers! You came, so I wished to let you hear my call. Umar said to him: Your land, O company of Tihamah people, is hot, so cool, then cool - two or three times - then call, then come to me with the second call."
"1993","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Sirin: he said: Ibn Mas'ud said to his companions: I will not spare you the time. He said: He prayed Zuhr with them - I think he said: when the sun declined."
"1994","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Ikrimah: he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was on a journey and wished to rest at midday in his station, and the shadow was a handspan, he prayed Zuhr."
"1995","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim: he said: It was narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) never alighted at a station on a journey then departed until he prayed Zuhr, and the soonest he would pray it was when the sun declined."
"1996","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: he said: It used to be said: When the sun inclines, a man on a journey should not leave his station."
"1997","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: Nafi' informed me that when Ibn Umar was told of much (distance) on a journey and the sun had declined while he was in his station, he would ride before praying, travel miles, halt, then pray Zuhr."
"1998","From Abdullah ibn Kathir, from Shu'bah ibn al-Hajjaj, from a man of Banu Dabbah: he said: I heard Anas ibn Malik say: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) alighted at a station, he would not depart until the saddles were untied."
"1999","From Malik, from Rabi'ah ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad: that he said: I found people only praying Zuhr in the afternoon."
"2000","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: I asked Ata about the decline of the sun, and he said: Its decline is its inclining. I said to Ata: If I stand for Zuhr and pray it hastening in it before the sun passes the meridian, and I do not bow until it has passed - he said: I do not like that. Then he recited: At the decline of the sun."
"2001","Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri: he said: Anas ibn Malik informed me: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray Asr, then one going to al-Awali would go while the sun was high. Al-Zuhri said: Al-Awali is two or three miles away. He said: I think he said: and four. From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from A'ishah, the like of it."
"2002","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray Asr before the sun left his chamber, rising. From Malik, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from A'ishah, the like of it."
"2003","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah: he said: A'ishah narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray the Asr prayer while the sun was in her chamber before it appeared, and the shade had not appeared from her chamber."
"2004","Sulayman ibn Musa said: It was reported to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) says: Pray the Asr prayer in the time a rider travels to Dhu al-Hulayfah, six miles."
"2005","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He who misses the Asr prayer, it is as if he has been bereft of his family and wealth. He said: Ibn Umar used to regard it as the middle prayer."
"2006","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: Nafi' informed me that Ibn Umar used to say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: He who misses Asr, it is as if he has been bereft of his family and wealth. I said to Nafi': Until the sun sets? He said: Yes."
"2007","From Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father: he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote: Pray while the sun is white and clear, as long as a rider travels two farsakhs, until the sun sets."
"2008","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from A'ishah: she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray Asr when the sun left my chamber, and my chamber was spacious."
"2009","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid: I heard Amr ibn Maymun al-Awdi, while I was leaving the mosque in the governorship of Bishr ibn Marwan: he said: Have you prayed Asr? He said: I said: I have just now prayed Zuhr? He said: I used to pray Asr with Umar at this time."
"2010","From Malik, from Ishaq ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Talhah, from Anas ibn Malik: he said: We would pray Asr, then a man would go out to Banu Amr ibn Awf, and would find them praying Asr."
"2011","From Malik, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman: he said: We entered upon Anas ibn Malik after Zuhr, and he went forward to pray Asr. When he finished, we reminded him of hastening the prayer, or he mentioned it. He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: That is the prayer of the hypocrites - three times - one of them sits until, when the sun yellows and is between the two horns, or upon the horn of Satan, he stands and pecks four, remembering Allah in them only little."
"2012","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abdullah ibn Abi Yazid: he said: I saw Ibn Abbas pray Asr sometimes at the time Zuhr is prayed, and pray Zuhr sometimes at the time of Asr."
"2013","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: Ibrahim ibn Maysarah informed me, from Tawus: that he would delay Asr until the sun yellowed greatly. I said to Ibrahim: A matter you saw? He said: Rather he would count for that - he would stay a day or two or three in Makkah to pray. Thus Ibn Jurayj said: Ibn Tawus would hasten it once and delay it once."
"2014","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: I said to Ata: What time is most beloved to you for me to pray Asr, as imam and alone? He said: Hastening it."
"2015","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: I said to Nafi': When would Ibn Umar pray Asr? He said: While the sun was white, unchanged; one traveling fast would travel five miles before night."
"2016","From Ma'mar, from Ali ibn Zayd ibn Jud'an, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri: he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led us in the Asr prayer one day in daylight."
"2017","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: Muhammad informed me that Umar ibn al-Khattab found al-Munkadir praying after Asr, so he sat beside him with the stick with him. He said: What is this prayer? Leave; I missed two rak'ahs of Asr. He said: When one of you misses Asr or part of it, let him not prolong until the yellowness of the sun overtakes him."
"2018","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin and Abu Qilabah: they both would make Asr late."
"2019","From Ma'mar, from Khalid al-Hadhdha: that al-Hasan, Muhammad ibn Sirin and Abu Qilabah would make Asr late."
"2020","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid: that Ibn Mas'ud would delay Asr."
"2021","From Ma'mar and Ibn Jurayj, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn Ka'b ibn Malik he informed him: that men of Banu Salamah would attend Maghrib with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then leave to their families while seeing the places of arrows."
"2022","From al-Thawri, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Jabir ibn Abdillah: he said: We would pray Maghrib with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then return to our dwellings, a mile away, while I could see the places of arrows."
"2023","From al-Thawri, from Imran ibn Muslim al-Ju'fi, from Suwayd ibn Ghaflah: he said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab say: Pray this prayer of yours - the Maghrib - while the roads are bright."
"2024","From al-Thawri, from Tariq ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Ibn al-Musayyib: he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote to the people of the garrison towns: Do not be outstripped in your breaking fast, nor waiting in your prayer for the intermingling of stars."
"2025","From Ibn Jurayj, from Sulayman ibn Musa: he said: I was informed that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would say: Pray Maghrib when the sun sets. Ibn Jurayj said: Tawus would pray it when it was the beginning of night."
"2026","Ibn Jurayj said: I said to Ata: What is the darkness of the night? He said: Its beginning when it enters. The most beloved to me is to pray Maghrib when the beginning of Maghrib enters."
"2027","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from one of Ibn Mas'ud's companions: that Ibn Mas'ud would pray Maghrib when the sun sets, saying: This, by Allah, is its time. He would not swear over any of the prayers but it."
"2028","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar: he said: I heard a son of Abdullah - meaning Abdullah ibn Mas'ud - say: Abdullah ibn Mas'ud would pray Maghrib when the edge of the sun sets, swearing it is the time which Allah the Blessed and Exalted mentioned: Establish the prayer at the decline of the sun to the darkness of the night. He said: He mentioned all the prayers but I did not retain them."
"2029","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Amr ibn Abi Amr, from Ubaydillah ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah, from Ibn Mas'ud: he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray Maghrib when the hastening one breaks fast."
"2030","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: Nafi' or another informed me that Ibn Umar used to say: No prayer is more fearful to me in missing than Maghrib."
"2031","From Ma'mar and Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Tawus, that Tawus used to say: There is no harm if the traveler and the sick person delay Maghrib, up to what the Hajj pilgrim delays it to pray it at Muzdalifah."
"2032","From Ibrahim ibn Yazid, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the sun set for him at Sarif, and he did not pray Maghrib until he entered Makkah."
"2033","From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, he said: I said to Salim: What is the farthest Ibn Umar delayed Maghrib? He said: From Dhat al-Jaysh to Dhat al-Ufuq, and between them are eight miles."
"2034","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Bakr ibn Ma'iz, he said: Rabi' ibn Khuthaym used to say to the mu'adhdhin on a cloudy evening: Delay the prayer into the darkness."
"2035","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me that Ibn Abbas left his land at Marr when the fasting people had broken their fast, heading for Madinah, and he did not pray Maghrib until he reached the highway at al-Zahran, and he combined it with Isha. It was said to him before that: The prayer! And he would say: Keep moving."
"2036","From Abdullah ibn Buhayr, he said: Wahb used to recognize the sun at al-Ruhbah and then ride, and he would not pray Maghrib except in his house; he did that more than once."
"2037","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Jibril came to him instituting the prayer, and he prayed every prayer at two times, except Maghrib, which he prayed at one time when the sun set."
"2038","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Were it not that I would burden my Ummah, I would have commanded them to use the siwak with ablution, and were it not that I would burden my Ummah, I would have delayed the Isha prayer to a third of the night or to half the night. For Allah - or he said: our Lord, blessed and exalted - descends to the lowest heaven and says: Who asks Me, that I may give him? Who seeks My forgiveness, that I may forgive him? Who calls upon Me, that I may answer him?"
"2039","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Were it not that I would burden the believers, I would have commanded them to use the siwak for every ablution, and to delay Isha - meaning al-Atamah."
"2040","From Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, he said: Umar wrote to Abu Musa: Pray the Isha prayer between you and a third of the night; if you delay, then up to half the night, and do not be among the heedless. From al-Thawri, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, the same."
"2041","From Ma'mar, from Ja'far ibn Burqan, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote: Pray the Isha prayer when the whiteness of the horizon has gone, between you and a third of the night; and whatever you hasten after the horizon has gone is better."
"2042","From Thawr ibn Yazid, he said: I heard Makhul saying: Ubadah ibn al-Samit and Shaddad ibn Aws used to pray the last Isha when the redness had gone. Makhul said: And that is the shafaq."
"2043","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I heard Ibn Abbas saying: The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) delayed Isha one night until the people slept and woke, and slept and woke. Then Umar ibn al-Khattab stood up and said: The prayer! Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out, as if I am looking at him now, water dripping from his head, placing his hand (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the side of his head, and he said: Were it not that I would burden my Ummah, I would have commanded them to pray it like this."
"2044","From Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ata, he said: I heard Ibn Abbas saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) delayed the Isha prayer one night, then he came out with his head dripping water, and said: Were it not that I would burden my Ummah, I would have loved to pray this prayer at this time."
"2045","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: al-Mughirah ibn Hakim informed me, from Umm Kulthum bint Abi Bakr, she informed him, from Aishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) delayed one night until most of the night had gone and the people of the mosque had slept. Then he came out and prayed, and said: Indeed it is its time, were it not that I would burden my Ummah."
"2046","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi' informed me, he said: Abdullah ibn Umar informed me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was occupied one night and delayed it until we slept, then woke, then slept, then woke. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us and said: There is no one among the people of the earth waiting for this prayer except you."
"2047","Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) delayed Isha one night, so Umar called out to him and said: The women and children have slept. So he came out to them and said: No one among the people of the earth is waiting for this prayer except you. Al-Zuhri said: And on that day none prayed except those in Madinah."
"2048","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said: It is more beloved to me to pray it, as imam or alone, delaying it as the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed it one night. But if that is hard on you and on the people, then pray it in the middle, neither hastened nor delayed. I said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote to Abd al-Aziz ibn Abd Allah a stern letter forbidding that the last Isha be prayed until the shafaq disappears, and mentioning in his letter that it had reached him that some people pray it before the shafaq disappears, and ordering them strictly in that."
"2049","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi' informed me that Ibn Umar did not care whether he prayed it early or delayed it, as long as sleep did not overcome him past its time."
"2050","From Ibn Jurayj, from Sulayman ibn Musa, he said: I was informed that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: Pray Isha after the shafaq disappears, between you and half the night."
"2051","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Abi Yazid informed me that he heard Ibn Abbas saying: There is no harm in delaying al-Atamah."
"2052","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, he said: We went out with Makhul to Makkah. He said: Thawr ibn Yazid would call the adhan for him, and he would order him not to call for Isha until the redness had gone, saying: It is the shafaq."
"2053","From Abdullah ibn Nafi', from his father, that Ibn Umar used to say: The shafaq is the redness."
"2054","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibrahim ibn Maysarah narrated to us, he said: I saw Tawus pray Maghrib and circumambulate seven circuits, then bow two rak'ahs, then pray the last Isha, then leave. He said: And he was at Mina: when he prayed Maghrib he would bow two rak'ahs, then pray the last Isha, then leave. He said: And I know that only before the shafaq had set."
"2055","From Amir, from Asim ibn Sulayman, he said: Anas ibn Malik, when he wanted to pray Isha, would say to a boy of his or a freedman of his: Look, have the two horizons become level?"
"2056","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Were it not for the weakness of the weak and the sickness of the sick, I would have delayed the Isha prayer."
"2057","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I saw Mu'awiyah pray Maghrib, then I would only circumambulate seven or seventy circuits before he would come out and pray Isha, and the shafaq had not disappeared. He said: And Ata used to say: Pray Isha before the shafaq disappears. Ata said: And I sometimes circumambulate seven circuits after Maghrib, then pray Isha."
"2058","From Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Ibrahim, he said: I saw Tawus pray Maghrib, then circumambulate seven circuits once, then pray Isha, then leave."
"2059","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: Pray Isha between you and a third of the night; whoever sleeps after a third of the night, may his eye not sleep."
"2060","From al-Thawri, from Ibrahim ibn Abd al-A'la, from Suwayd ibn Ghaflah, he said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab saying: Pray Isha before the sick person sleeps and the worker becomes lazy."
"2061","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Khaythamah, he said: One who heard Abdullah informed me, he saying: From the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): There is no conversation after the Isha prayer except for one praying or a traveler."
"2062","From al-Thawri, from Awf, from Abu al-Minhal, from Abu Barzah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he disliked - or forbade - sleep before it and talk after it."
"2063","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Sulayman ibn Mushir, from Kharashah ibn al-Hurr, he said: I saw Umar ibn al-Khattab beating the people for conversing after it."
"2064","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Yahya ibn Ja'dah, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab passed by one conversing at night and greeted him, and said: By Him besides Whom there is no god, there is no god but Allah, and I enjoin upon you the fear of Allah."
"2065","From Ma'mar, from al-A'mash, from Sulayman ibn Mushir, from Kharashah ibn al-Hurr al-Fazari, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab saw people conversing after Isha and dispersed them with the stick, saying: Conversation at its beginning and sleep at its end?"
"2066","From Ma'mar, from Aban, he said: Abu Khalaf the blind asked Anas about a woman of his family who sleeps before the last Isha. He said: I order her not to pray after sleep, meaning she should not sleep until she prays. He said: Some of her family only order that she be woken when the mu'adhdhin calls. He said: Order her. We said: Order the one you ordered to wake her not to let her sleep."
"2067","From Yahya ibn al-Ala, from al-A'mash, from Abu Wa'il, he said: I sought Hudhayfah, and he said: Why did you seek me? He said: I said: For the hadith. So he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, used to warn against talking after the sleep prayer."
"2068","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: One whom I trust narrated to me, from Aishah, that she heard Urwah talking after al-Atamah, so she said: What is this talk after al-Atamah? I never saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sleeping before it at all, nor talking after it; either praying and gaining, or sleeping and safe. From al-Thawri, from Aban, from Anas, similar, with the addition: This verse was revealed about that, they remember Allah: Their sides forsake their beds."
"2069","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Uthman ibn Muhammad informed me, from a man of Banu Salimah, raising it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: Beware of conversation after the last Isha, and when donkeys bray at night, seek refuge in Allah from Satan."
"2070","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Ata saying: When donkeys bray at night, say: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate; I seek refuge in Allah from the accursed Satan."
"2071","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: He used to dislike sleep before Isha and conversation after it."
"2072","From Abdullah ibn Umar, and Malik ibn Anas, from Nafi', and Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Whoever sleeps before Isha, may his eye not sleep."
"2073","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Layth, from Mujahid, he said: There is no harm in conversation after Isha for jurisprudence."
"2074","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: That I sleep past Isha is more beloved to me than that I engage in idle talk after it."
"2075","From Ibn Jurayj, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: That I sleep through the Isha which the Bedouins call al-Atamah is more beloved to me than that I engage in idle talk after it."
"2076","From Ibn Jurayj, from Nafi', that Ibn Umar would sometimes sleep through the last Isha, and would order his family to wake him."
"2077","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah, from his grandmother, and she was a concubine of Ali, she said: Ali would eat dinner, then sleep with his clothes on before Isha."
"2078","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, he said: He used to complete the Quran in two nights, and sleep between Maghrib and Isha in Ramadan."
"2079","From Ja'far ibn Sulayman, from a man of the people of Makkah, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, he said: I was talking after the last Isha, so Aishah called out to me: Will you not give rest to your two scribes, O Urayyah?! The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not sleep before it, nor talk after it."
"2080","Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: It reached me that Abu Hurayrah said: Whoever fears sleeping before the Isha prayer, there is no harm in praying before the shafaq disappears."
"2081","From al-Thawri, from Abdullah ibn Abi Labid, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is the Isha prayer, so let not the Bedouins overcome you over the name of your prayer, for they darken from the camels."
"2082","From Ibn Uyaynah, he said: Abdullah ibn Abi Labid narrated to us, from Abu Salamah, from Ibn Umar, he said: I heard him saying on the minbar: Verily the Bedouins must not overcome you over the name of your prayer; verily it is Isha, and they darken from the camels - or he said: the camels."
"2083","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was informed from Tamim ibn Ghaylan al-Thaqafi, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Abd al-Rahman! Do not be overcome over the name of your prayer, for Allah named it Isha, and the Bedouins only named it al-Atamah because of the dark milking of their camels."
"2084","From Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Rawwad, he said: Ibn Umar, when he heard them saying al-Atamah, would become angry and shout at them."
"2085","From Ma'mar, he said: It reached me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let not the Bedouins overcome you over the name of your prayer - meaning Isha."
"2086","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed Fajr one day, then prayed it late the next morning, then said: What is between these two is time."
"2087","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, that a man stood up to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him about the time of Fajr. So he ordered his caller, who established it at the rising of dawn. Then he ordered him after that not to establish until he orders him. So he left him until it was very bright, then he ordered him, so he stood and prayed with him. Then he said: Where is the asker about the prayer time? So the man stood. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Did you witness the two prayers with us? He said: Yes. He said: What is between the two prayers is time."
"2088","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Kathir ibn Kathir informed me, from Ali ibn Abd Allah, from Zayd ibn Harithah, that a man asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the time of the Fajr prayer, so he said: Pray it with us today and tomorrow. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was at the valley of Namirah at al-Juhfah, he prayed it when the first dawn rose; then when he was at Dhi Tuwa he delayed it until the people said: Has the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) been taken, or did he pray? So he prayed it just before the sun, then turned to the people and said: What did you say? They said: We said: If only we had prayed. He said: If you had done so, punishment would have befallen you. Then he called the asker and said: Its time is between my two prayers."
"2089","From al-Thawri, and Ibn Uyaynah, from Muhammad ibn Ajlan, from Asim ibn Umar ibn Qatadah, from Mahmud ibn Labid, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Pray the morning prayer in brightness."
"2090","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, he said: Abdullah used to brighten the morning prayer."
"2091","From Yahya ibn al-Ala, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, he said: We prayed the morning prayer with Ibn Mas'ud, and we kept looking around when we left. He said: What is wrong with you?! So we said: We think the sun is rising. He said: This, by Him besides Whom there is no god, is the appointed time of this prayer: Establish the prayer at the decline of the sun to the darkness of the night - so this is the decline of the sun, and this is the darkness of the night."
"2092","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me that he heard a son of Abdullah ibn Mas'ud saying: Abdullah ibn Mas'ud used to pray Fajr in darkness as Ibn al-Zubayr prays it in darkness, and he would pray Maghrib when the sun sets, saying: By Allah, they are indeed as Allah said: To the darkness of the night, and the recitation of dawn, indeed the recitation of dawn is witnessed."
"2093","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Tawus said: Its time is when the dawn rises, and it was more beloved to him to brighten it."
"2094","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that he used to brighten the morning prayer."
"2095","From al-Thawri, from Sa'id ibn Ubayd, from Ali ibn Rabi'ah, he said: I heard Ali saying to his mu'adhdhin: Brighten, brighten - meaning the Fajr prayer."
"2096","From al-Thawri, from Ubayd ibn Iyas, he said: I heard Sa'id ibn Jubayr saying to the mu'adhdhin: Brighten, brighten - meaning the Fajr prayer."
"2097","From al-Thawri, from Ubayd al-Mukattib, he said: Ibrahim said to me, and I was a mu'adhdhin: Brighten, brighten - meaning the Fajr prayer."
"2098","From Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash, from Abu al-Husayn, from Kharashah ibn al-Hurr, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab would pray the Fajr prayer in darkness and in brightness, and would pray it between that."
"2099","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What time is most beloved to you for me to pray Fajr as imam and alone? He said: When the second dawn bursts, then lengthen the recitation, the bowing, and the prostration, until you leave it with the dawn bright, and completing the people. It has reached me that Umar ibn al-Khattab used to pray it when the second dawn bursts, and he would recite Surah Yusuf in one of them."
"2100","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from Abu al-Aliyah, he said: Umar wrote: Pray Fajr when the dawn rises and the stars are intertwined in darkness, and lengthen the recitation."
"2101","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Mansur ibn Hayyan, from Amr ibn Maymun al-Awdi, he said: I used to pray Fajr with Umar ibn al-Khattab, and if my son had been beside me, I would not have recognized his face."
"2102","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: Laqit narrated to me that he heard Ibn al-Zubayr saying: I used to pray with Umar, then leave and not recognize the face of my companion."
"2103","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: I used to pray Fajr with Ibn al-Zubayr, then go to Ajyad and relieve my need, while it was still dark."
"2104","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', he said: Ibn Umar would pray Fajr with Ibn al-Zubayr, then return to his house right after the prayer, because Ibn al-Zubayr would pray at night - or he said: in darkness."
"2105","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: Whoever prays the Fajr prayer at night must repeat it when the dawn rises, and repeat the iqamah. From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, the same as the hadith of Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi'."
"2106","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi' informed me that Ibn Umar, when Fajr became clear to him without doubt, would make his camel kneel and pray Fajr."
"2107","From Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Rawwad, from Nafi', he said: When al-Hajjaj came against Ibn al-Zubayr, he prayed Fajr at Mina, then brightened it greatly. So Ibn Umar sent to him: What makes you delay the prayer to this people? He said: We are a fighting, fearful people. So Ibn Umar replied to him: There is no fear upon you to pray the prayer at its time, so do not delay it to this time. And Ibn Umar prayed with him."
"2108","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: And the recitation of dawn? He said: It is Fajr. I said: Was witnessed? He said: The angels and the good witness it."
"2109","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: I rose for Fajr before the dawn rose, and did not bow until the dawn rose. He said: I do not like that. He said: And the recitation of dawn, indeed the recitation of dawn is witnessed."
"2110","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Hind bint al-Harith, from Umm Salamah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), she said: There were women who would attend the Fajr prayer with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then leave wrapped in their cloaks, unrecognized due to the darkness. She said: And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he gave salam, would remain in his place a little. And they thought that was so the women would leave before the men."
"2111","From Ma'mar, from Zayd ibn Aslam, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Brighten the Fajr prayer, for it is greater in reward."
"2112","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Anas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When dinner is brought near and the prayer is called, begin with dinner, then pray."
"2113","From al-Thawri, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from Aishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the prayer is established and dinner is placed, begin with dinner."
"2114","From Ma'mar, from Ja'far ibn Burqan, he said: Maymun ibn Mihran invited us to food, and the prayer was called. So we stood up and left his food, and it seemed he was hurt by it, so he said: By Allah! Something like this happened in the time of Umar, and he began with the food."
"2115","From Amir, from Abu Asim al-Absi, from Yasar ibn Numayr, treasurer of Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: Yasar invited us to food, and we wanted to stand when the prayer came, so he said: Umar used to order us, when the prayer came and the food was placed, to begin with the food."
"2116","From Ma'mar, from Thabit, from Anas, he said: I was with Ubayy ibn Ka'b, and Ibn Talhah, and men of the Ansar, and the prayer was called while we were at food of ours. Anas said: So I turned to go out, but they held me back and said: An Iraqi fatwa? And they blamed me for that until I sat down."
"2117","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, that Jabir ibn Abd Allah said: When one of you is at his dinner or his food and the prayer is called, let him not hurry from it until he finishes."
"2118","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi' informed me, he said: Sometimes we would meet Ibn Umar while he was fasting, and dinner would be presented to him after the Maghrib prayer had been called, then it would be established while he hears - meaning the prayer - yet he would not leave his dinner nor hurry until he finished his dinner. Then he would go out and pray, saying: The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: Do not hurry from your dinners when they are presented to you."
"2119","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', that Ibn Umar would be at his food while hearing the recitation of the imam, and he would not stand until he finished his food."
"2120","Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The one who misses the Asr prayer, it is as if his family and wealth have been destroyed. He said: And Abdullah thought it is the middle prayer."
"2121","From al-Thawri, from Asim, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, he said: I said to Ubaydah: Ask Ali about the middle prayer. So he asked him, and he said: We used to think it is the Asr prayer, until I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying on the day of al-Khandaq: They distracted us from the middle prayer, the Asr prayer; may Allah fill their graves and their bellies with fire."
"2122","From Ma'mar, from al-A'mash, from Ali, that he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on the day of al-Ahzab: May Allah fill their graves and their houses with fire, as they distracted us from the middle prayer until the sun set, and on that day he had not prayed Zuhr and Asr until the sun set."
"2123","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Abu al-Duha, from Shutayr ibn Shakl al-Absi, he said: I heard Ali saying: When it was the day of al-Ahzab, we prayed Asr between Maghrib and Isha. May Allah fill their graves and their bellies with fire; they distracted us from the middle prayer, the Asr prayer; may Allah fill their graves and their bellies with fire. From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from a man of Abd al-Qays, from Ali, that he said: It is Asr."
"2124","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: I asked Ubaydah about the middle prayer, and he said: It is Asr."
"2125","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Khuthaym, from Ibn Labibah, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: It is Asr."
"2126","From Sa'id ibn Bashir, from Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyib, from Zayd ibn Thabit, he said: It is Zuhr."
"2127","From Malik, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from Ibn Yarbu', he said: I heard Zayd ibn Thabit saying: It is Zuhr."
"2128","From Ma'mar, from Sa'id ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Jahshi, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm, he said: Zayd ibn Thabit sent his freedman Harmalah to Aishah asking her about the middle prayer. She said: It is Zuhr. She said: And Zayd used to say: It is Zuhr, and I do not know whether he took it from her or from another."
"2129","From Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, he said: I read in the mushaf of Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her: Guard the prayers and the middle prayer, and the Asr prayer, and stand for Allah devoutly."
"2130","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi' informed me that Hafsah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), gave a mushaf to a freedman of hers to copy it, and said: When you reach this verse, Guard the prayers and the middle prayer, inform me. When he reached it, he came to her, so she wrote with her hand: Guard the prayers and the middle prayer, and the Asr prayer, and stand for Allah devoutly. He said: And Umm Humayd bint Abd al-Rahman asked Aishah about the middle prayer, so she said: We used to recite it in the first covenant, at the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Guard the prayers and the middle prayer and the Asr prayer and stand for Allah devoutly. He said: Ibn Jurayj mentioned, he said: Abd al-Malik ibn Abd al-Rahman informed me, from his mother Umm Humayd, that she asked Aishah."
"2131","From Dawud ibn Qays, that he heard Abdullah ibn Rafi' saying: Umm Salamah ordered me to write a mushaf for her, and said: When you reach, Guard the prayers and the middle prayer, inform me. So I informed her, and she said: Write: Guard the prayers and the middle prayer and the Asr prayer and stand for Allah devoutly."
"2132","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I asked Ata about the middle prayer. He said: I think it is Fajr; do you not hear His saying: And the recitation of dawn, indeed the recitation of dawn is witnessed."
"2133","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus in his hadith: I made it middle, and it was between the night and the day."
"2134","From Ja'far ibn Sulayman, from Awf, from Abu Raja, that he heard Ibn Abbas saying: It is the morning prayer."
"2135","From Abu Ja'far al-Razi, from al-Rabi' ibn Anas, from Abu al-Aliyah, he said: We prayed the morning prayer with the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). When we finished, I said: Which prayer is the middle prayer? He said: The one you just prayed."
"2136","From Ibn Abi Sabrah, from Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu Nasrah al-Ghifari, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led us in the Asr prayer. When he finished it, he turned and said: This prayer was enjoined upon those before you but they refused it, and it was heavy upon them, and it is distinguished over others by twenty-six degrees. Abu Sa'id said: Thus al-Dabari said Abu Nasrah, with Sad and Nun in his origin, and so al-Dabari said; and the correct is Abu Basrah."
"2137","Ma'mar informed us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: One of you remains in prayer as long as he keeps waiting for the prayer, and the angels keep praying for one of you as long as he is in the mosque, saying: O Allah, forgive him; O Allah, have mercy on him."
"2138","From Ma'mar, from Hammam ibn Munabbih, that he heard Abu Hurayrah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: One of you remains in prayer as long as he waits for it, and the angels keep praying for one of you as long as he is in the mosque, saying: O Allah, forgive him; O Allah, have mercy on him, as long as he does not make hadath. A man of Hadramawt said: What is hadath, O Abu Hurayrah? He said: A fart, silent or loud."
"2139","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: When is the neglect of Fajr? He said: Until its rising becomes clear. I said to him: When is the neglect of Zuhr? He said: There is no neglect for it until the sun enters yellowness. I said: And Asr? He said: Until the sun enters yellowness."
"2140","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: It was said: The Isha prayer is between us and half the night, and what is beyond that is neglect, and Maghrib is similar. He said: Neglect for it is until the first half of the night."
"2141","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me that Ibn Abbas left his land at Marr when the fasting person had broken his fast, heading for Madinah, and he did not pray Maghrib until he reached the highway at al-Zahran, combining between it and Isha, and it was said to him: The prayer!"
"2142","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As, he said: When the sun declines from the belly of the sky, the Zuhr prayer is attainable until Asr comes, and the Asr prayer is attainable until the shafaq goes, and what is after that is excess. And the Isha prayer is attainable until half the night, and what is after that is excess. And the Fajr prayer is attainable until the horn of the sun rises, and what is after that is excess."
"2143","From al-Thawri, from Uthman ibn Mawhib, he said: I heard Abu Hurayrah, and a man asked him about neglect in prayer. He said: That you delay them to the time of the one after it; whoever does that has neglected."
"2144","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from al-Qasim ibn Ghannam, from some of his mothers or grandmothers, from Umm Farwah, and she had pledged the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked: Which deeds are best? He said: Prayer at the beginning of its time."
"2145","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: I prayed some prayers neglecting them, but they did not escape me. He said: Then do not prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness."
"2146","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: The daytime prayer, Zuhr and Asr, is not missed until night, and the nighttime prayer, Maghrib and Isha, is not missed until day, and the time of Fajr is not missed until the sun rises."
"2147","From Ibn Abi Sabrah, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Nawfal ibn Mu'awiyah, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: That one of you be left without his family and wealth is better for him than missing the time of a prayer."
"2148","From Dawud ibn Ibrahim, he said: I asked Tawus: When is the Isha prayer missed? He said: Until Fajr, without taking that as a habit, and do not ever say that you are better than anyone."
"2149","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Tawus would not pray Maghrib at Muzdalifah until the shafaq had gone. He said: And Tawus used to say: Zuhr and Asr are not missed until night, and Maghrib and Isha are not missed until dawn, and Fajr is not missed until the sun rises. From Ma'mar, from one who heard Ikrimah, saying the same as the saying of Tawus."
"2150","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever catches a rak'ah of Asr before the sun sets has caught it, and whoever catches a rak'ah of Fajr before the sun rises has caught it."
"2151","From Ibn Abi Sabrah, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Ya'la ibn Muslim, from Talq ibn Habib, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: One of you prays, yet what he has missed of its time is worse for him than his family and wealth. [obscure wording]"
"2152","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Ibn Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The time of Zuhr is to Asr, and Asr to Maghrib, and Maghrib to Isha, and Isha to Fajr. Al-Thawri said: And some jurists used to say: Zuhr and Asr until night, and Maghrib and Isha are not missed until dawn, and Fajr is not missed until the sun rises."
"2153","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Whoever catches a rak'ah of Fajr before the sun rises has caught it."
"2154","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Dhakwan, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: Whoever catches a rak'ah of Fajr before the sun rises has caught it, and whoever catches two rak'ahs of Asr before the sun sets has caught it."
"2155","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: Whoever catches a rak'ah of Fajr before the sun rises has caught it, and whoever catches two rak'ahs of Asr before the sun sets has caught it."
"2156","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, that al-Miswar ibn Makhramah visited Ibn Abbas and spoke to him while he was reclining on a pillow. Then Ibn Abbas slept, and al-Miswar ibn Makhramah slipped away from him. He did not wake until morning, so he said to his slave: Do you think I can pray before the sun comes out: four, meaning Isha, and three, meaning witr, and two, meaning Fajr, and one, meaning a rak'ah of Fajr? He said: Yes, so he prayed them."
"2157","From Abdullah ibn Muharrar, from Qatadah, from Abu al-Jawza, he said: al-Miswar ibn Makhramah visited Ibn Abbas, and I covered Ibn Abbas with a pillow. He slept on it, and al-Miswar ibn Makhramah spoke at his place a little, then left, and Ibn Abbas slept past Isha and witr until morning. So he said to his slave: Do you think I can pray Isha and witr, and the two rak'ahs of Fajr, and a rak'ah before the sun rises? He said: Yes! He said: So Ibn Abbas prayed: Isha, then prayed witr, and prayed the two rak'ahs of Fajr, then prayed Fajr, when the sun was almost rising."
"2158","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me, and Abd al-Rahman ibn Amir, from Ata ibn Yuhannis, that he heard Abu Hurayrah saying: If you fear missing Asr, shorten the first two rak'ahs; if you beat the night with them, then complete the last two, and lengthen them if you wish."
"2159","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me, from Ata ibn Yuhannis, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: If you fear missing Fajr, hasten with the first rak'ah against the sun; if you beat the sun with it, then do not hurry with the last to complete it."
"2160","From Ma'mar, from Budayl al-Uqayli, he said: It reached me that when the servant prays at its time, a bright light rises for it in the sky, and it says: You guarded me, may Allah guard you. And when he prays it at other than its time, it is folded as a worn garment is folded, and his face is struck with it."
"2161","From Ziyad ibn al-Fayyad, he said: I heard Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami saying: Were it not that a man prayed two rak'ahs before the morning prayer and then died, he would have prayed the morning."
"2162","From Ma'mar, he said: I heard one saying: When he fears the sun rising, he shortens the first rak'ah and lengthens the last if he wishes."
"2163","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned from Khaybar, he traveled a night until, at the end of the night, he turned from the road, then camped, and said: Who will guard the prayer for us? So Bilal said: I, O Messenger of Allah! Then he sat guarding them, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions slept. While Bilal was sitting, his eyes overcame him, and nothing woke them except the heat of the sun. So they panicked, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Did you sleep, O Bilal? He said: O Messenger of Allah, my soul was taken as yours were taken. He said: Hasten your mounts and move from the place where heedlessness befell you. Then he prayed Fajr with them. When he finished, he said: Whoever forgets a prayer, let him pray it when he remembers, for Allah the Exalted says: And establish the prayer for My remembrance. He said: I said to al-Zuhri: Did it reach you that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited it li-dhikri? He said: Yes. Ma'mar said: al-Hasan used to narrate a similar hadith, mentioning that they bowed two rak'ahs, then he prayed Fajr with them."
"2164","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me that the Prophet, while he was in one of his journeys, traveled their night until, at the end of the night, they alighted to rest. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who will wake us for Fajr? Bilal said: I. So Bilal pillowed his camel's foreleg, and they did not wake until the sun rose. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood, performed ablution, bowed two rak'ahs in his resting place, then traveled a while, then prayed Fajr. I said to Ata: Which journey was it? He said: I do not know."
"2165","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Sa'd ibn Ibrahim informed me, from Ata ibn Yasar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) slept and did not wake except to the heat of the sun. So he traveled until he passed the valley, and said: We do not pray where Satan made us forget. He said: So he prayed two rak'ahs, and ordered Bilal, who called the adhan, and the iqamah was made, and he prayed."
"2166","From Uthman ibn Matar, from Sa'id, from Qatadah, from Abdullah ibn Rabah al-Ansari, from Abu Qatadah, he said: And Ma'mar informed us, from Qatadah, that Abu Qatadah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me, while we were traveling a night and sleep took him: Turn from the road and halt. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) halted and we halted. He said: Each man of us pillowed his mount's foreleg. We did not wake until the sun shone, and we woke only to the sound of the surad bird. So we said: O Messenger of Allah, we have perished. He said: You have not perished; prayer does not escape the sleeper, it only escapes the wakeful. He said: So he performed ablution and ordered Bilal, who called the adhan, and he prayed two rak'ahs. Then he moved from that place, then ordered him, who made the iqamah, and he prayed Fajr with us."
"2167","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Isma'il ibn Muslim, from al-Hasan, from Imran ibn Husayn, he said: When we slept past the prayer and woke, we said: O Messenger of Allah! Shall we not pray such-and-such prayers? He said: Does our Lord forbid us usury and accept it from us? Negligence is only in wakefulness."
"2168","From Ibn Uyaynah, and Yahya ibn al-Ala, from al-A'mash, from Zayd ibn Wahb, he said: A man came to Ibn Mas'ud and said: I slept past the Fajr prayer until the sun rose. Abdullah said: Go and perform ablution as best as you do, and pray as best as you pray. Then he repeated the saying to him, so he said the same to him. His soul did not leave him, due to the enormity of his sin in himself, until he worshipped Allah when he left him relieved. He said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman! And he repeated the saying to him. He said: So Abdullah took his hand and said: It is only said to you to do: Go and perform ablution as best as you do, and pray as best as you pray."
"2169","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, about a man who forgot Zuhr until he prayed Asr, he said: Asr has passed for him, and he prays Zuhr. Al-Thawri said, and he says: When he prays with people a prayer while he has not prayed the one before it, he repeats both, unless he is forgetful, then it suffices him."
"2170","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ali ibn Husayn, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon Ali and Fatimah while they were sleeping, and said: Will you not pray? Ali said: O Messenger of Allah! Our souls are in Allah's hand; when He wills to send them, He sends them. So he turned away from them, saying: And man is most contentious."
"2171","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever forgets a prayer, let him pray it when he remembers, for Allah says: And establish the prayer for My remembrance."
"2172","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: He prays it when he remembers it, and does not prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness. Ata said: And if he forgets the prayers of two days, he prays the prayers of those two days when he remembers: And remember your Lord when you forget."
"2173","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Tawus used to say about a man who forgot a daytime prayer until he remembered it at night: Let him pray it when he remembers it."
"2174","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, he said: Let him pray it when he remembers it."
"2175","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: Pray it when you remember it - meaning Ibrahim and all from whom this is reported - even if that is at a time when prayer is disliked."
"2176","From Ma'mar and al-Thawri, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin: Abu Bakrah came to them in their garden and slept through the Asr prayer. He said: we thought he had prayed, but he had not prayed. So he stood, performed ablution, and did not pray until the sun had set."
"2177","From al-Thawri, from Sa'd ibn Ishaq ibn Ka'b ibn Ujrah, from a man of the offspring of Ka'b ibn Ujrah: he slept through Fajr until the sun rose. He said: I got up to pray, then he called me and seated me - meaning Ka'b - until the sun rose high and turned white, then he said: stand and pray."
"2178","From Ma'mar, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Ibn al-Musayyab, regarding a man who forgot a prayer until the time of the next one entered, and feared that if he prays the first prayer this one would be missed. He said: he should pray this prayer whose missing he fears, and he has not wasted twice. From Ma'mar, from al-Hasan, similar. Abu Bakr said: and al-Thawri holds this view."
"2179","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, regarding a man who forgot Isha or slept through it until the time of Subh, and it was said to him: if he begins with Isha, Subh will be missed. He said: let him begin with Isha, even if the Subh prayer is missed."
"2180","From Malik, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar: whoever forgets a prayer and remembers only while he is with the imam, when the imam gives salam let him pray the prayer he forgot, then pray the other one after it."
"2181","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, regarding a man who joined people praying Asr while thinking it was Zuhr. He said: he prays. He said: he prays Zuhr, then Asr, and he does not repeat what he prayed, so as to give precedence to what Allah gave precedence."
"2182","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Kathir ibn Aflah: I reached Madinah while they were praying Asr, and I had not prayed Zuhr. He said: I prayed with them thinking it was Zuhr. When I finished, I learned it was Asr. So I prayed Zuhr, then I prayed Asr, then I prayed Asr. Then I asked in Madinah, and all of them instructed me with what I had done. Ibn Sirin said: and the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were there that day."
"2183","From a man, from Ibrahim, regarding a man who joined people in Asr while he had not prayed Zuhr. He said: Allah has prescribed Zuhr before Asr, so let him pray Zuhr then pray Asr. Sufyan said: and we say: if he prayed with people a prayer while he had not prayed the one before it, he repeats both together, except if he was forgetful, then it suffices him."
"2184","From Ibn Jurayh: Ata said: you caught Asr, so make the one you caught with the imam as Zuhr, and pray Asr after that. He said: he used to do that."
"2185","From Ibn Jurayj: Ata said: if he forgot Asr and remembered while in Maghrib that he had not prayed it, let him make it Asr. And if he remembered after finishing, let him pray Asr."
"2186","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, regarding a man who forgot a prayer until he remembers it during the other one. He said: if he has prayed part of it, he completes it, then prays the first one. Ma'mar said: al-Hasan said: he leaves and begins with the first. He mentioned it from al-Hasan."
"2187","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, regarding a man who slept through Zuhr until Asr while he was the imam of people, then prayed with them intending it as Zuhr while they intended Asr. He said: it counts for his prayer and he may rely on it, and they repeat Asr."
"2188","From al-Thawri, from Khalid, from Abu Qilabah: one prayer cannot be for different purposes."
"2189","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah and Ata al-Khurasani: Abu al-Darda came to the people of Hims while they were praying Isha, thinking it was Maghrib. When the imam gave salam, he stood and prayed another rak'ah, counting three as Maghrib and making the two rak'ahs voluntary, then prayed Isha after that. Ma'mar said, and al-Zuhri said: he repeats Maghrib and Isha."
"2190","From Ibn Jurayj: it was narrated to me from Ikrimah, the freedman of Ibn Abbas: Mu'adh ibn Jabal used to pray with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the prayer people call Atamah, then go and lead them in the latter Isha as well; it was voluntary for him and obligatory for them. From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Mu'adh ibn Jabal, similar."
"2191","From Ibn Jurayj: Tawus said: if you prayed in your house and found people in it (the prayer), pray with them; and if you find them in Maghrib, add a rak'ah to make it even."
"2192","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father: when a man comes to the night standing of Ramadan and has not prayed the obligatory prayer, he prays with them, counting it as the obligatory one."
"2193","He said: al-Thawri said, from Khalid, from Abu Qilabah: one prayer cannot be for different purposes."
"2194","From Ibn Jurayj: Sulayman ibn Musa asked Ata: I come to the people in the standing (prayer) in Ramadan, and two rak'ahs remain. He said: make them part of the latter Isha. Sulayman said: as an opinion? He said: yes, as an opinion. Sulayman said: how, while they are in voluntary prayer and I am in an obligatory one? He said: the congregation. From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from Anas, similar."
"2195","From Ma'mar, from Hammad: when one mixes the obligatory with the voluntary, it is like speech."
"2196","From Ibn Jurayj: Ata said: those of the past used to make the back of the saddle as a sutrah when they prayed. I said: how much has reached you? He said: the size of the back of the saddle. He said: a cubit. He said: and I heard al-Thawri giving fatwa with the saying of Ata."
"2197","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi': Ibn Umar would not pray except toward a sutrah. He said: the size of the back of his saddle was a cubit. He said: he would pray, and sometimes he would make his camel kneel across and pray toward it."
"2198","From Ibn Jurayj: Nafi' informed me that Ibn Umar, while traveling, would place his saddle and make its back a third of it when there was nothing else, or he would turn his mount and place it between himself and the qiblah and pray toward it."
"2199","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah: how much should a man place before him when praying? He said: like the back of a saddle; and while you pray, nothing that passes before you harms you."
"2200","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq: I heard al-Muhallab ibn Abi Sufrah say: one who heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed me, saying: when there is between you and the road the like of the back of a saddle, whoever passes over you does not harm you."
"2201","From Ibn Jurayj: Nafi', the freedman of Ibn Umar, informed me that Ibn Umar used to dislike stones in the mosque."
"2202","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi': Ibn Umar would not pray toward these milestones between Makkah and Madinah, which were of stone. It was said to him: why did you dislike that? He said: I likened them to idols."
"2203","From Hisham ibn Hassan: Anas ibn Sirin informed me that he saw Ibn Umar make his mount kneel between himself and the qiblah, then pray Maghrib and Isha."
"2204","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin: Ibn Umar led us in prayer with his mount between himself and the qiblah."
"2205","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would bring out a small spear with him on the day of Fitr and Adha to plant it and pray toward it."
"2206","From Isra'il, from Ibrahim ibn Abd al-A'la: he saw Suwayd ibn Ghaflah on the Makkah road making his camel kneel and praying toward it."
"2207","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar: a small spear used to be carried with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Eid, and he would pray toward it; and when he traveled it was carried with him and he would pray toward it."
"2208","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Abu al-Duha: I saw Ibn Umar praying toward his camel."
"2209","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abdullah, from al-Hasan: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed toward a camel, then took a hair from the top of its hump and said: nothing of what Allah has granted you as booty is lawful, not even the like of these hairs, except the fifth; then it is returned to you."
"2210","From Ibn Jurayj: Isma'il ibn Umayyah informed me, from Hurayth ibn Ammar, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): when one of you prays, let him pray toward something; if he finds nothing, let him set up a stick; if he finds no stick, let him draw a line before him, and nothing that passes before him will harm him."
"2211","From Ibn Jurayj: Abd al-Karim al-Jazari informed me that the spear was carried with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only so that he would pray toward it. From Isma'il ibn Umayyah, from Makhul, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar."
"2212","From Ma'mar, from Isma'il ibn Umayyah, the hadith raised to Abu Hurayrah: nothing harms you when there is a sutrah before you, even if it is thinner than a hair."
"2213","From al-Thawri, from Yazid ibn Yazid ibn Jabir, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah: when it is the size of the back of a saddle - or he said: the back of a saddle - even if it is the size of a hair, it suffices him."
"2214","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Mis'ar, from Abu Isma'il al-Saksaki: Abu Hurayrah said: the like of the back of a saddle in the thickness of a whip - meaning the sutrah."
"2215","From al-Thawri, from Simak ibn Harb, from Musa ibn Talhah: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked what screens the praying person from animals. He said: the like of the back of a saddle before him."
"2216","From al-Thawri: Tawus used to say: the like of the back of a saddle, or a stick if he has no back of a saddle with him."
"2217","From Ma'mar, from Abu Harun al-Abdi, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri: we used to screen ourselves with an arrow and a stone in prayer - or he said: one of us would screen himself with an arrow and a stone in prayer."
"2218","From Ja'far ibn Sulayman: Abu Harun al-Abdi informed me: I said to Abu Sa'id al-Khudri: what screens the praying person? He said: the like of the back of a saddle; a stone suffices for that, and an arrow which you plant before you."
"2219","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim: it used to be disliked for a man to pray toward a laid-down stick, or toward a reed, or toward a whip. He said: it does not suffice him until he plants it upright. Al-Thawri said: drawing a line is dearer to me than these stones on the road when it is not a cubit."
"2220","From Hushaym, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Iyas ibn Mu'awiyah, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr: when you are in open land and have something to plant, plant it before you; if you have nothing, draw a line before you."
"2221","From Ma'mar: I heard Qatadah asked about a reed, and reeds which a man places before him while praying. He said: it screens him when it is a cubit and a span."
"2222","From Ibn Jurayj: I said to Ata: what if I had with me a stick of a cubit, of which four fingers are in the ground, its clear part above the ground less than a cubit? He said: no, until its clear part above the ground is a cubit."
"2223","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin: I heard Shurayh say: the size of the back of a saddle; and whatever before you that screens you is more pleasing to yourself."
"2224","From a trustworthy man: Ibrahim ibn Abi Ablah informed me: one who saw Umar ibn al-Khattab informed me that he prayed toward his cap, making it a screen for himself."
"2225","From Dawud ibn Qays, that he heard Nafi' ibn Jubayr say: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: when one of you prays, let him pray toward a sutrah, and let him draw near to it, for Satan passes between them."
"2226","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Ayyub, from Muhammad ibn Sirin: Umar ibn al-Khattab saw a man praying with no sutrah before him, so he sat before him. He said: do not hasten from your prayer. When he finished, Umar said to him: when one of you prays, let him pray toward a sutrah, so that Satan does not come between him and his prayer."
"2227","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Safwan: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: when one of you prays, let him pray toward a sutrah."
"2228","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from al-Mughirah, from Abu Ubaydah, from Abdullah: none of you should pray while there is a gap between him and the qiblah."
"2229","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Yunus, from Abu Ishaq: I saw Abdullah ibn Mughaffal praying with about seven cubits between him and his sutrah."
"2230","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: it is said: the least that suffices you between you and the pillar is three cubits."
"2231","From Ibn Jurayj: Umar ibn al-Khattab passed by a youth praying. Umar said: boy, O boy - three times - until Umar saw that he had recognized his voice: advance to the pillar, let not Satan toy with your prayer; I do not say it as an opinion, but I heard it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"2232","From Amir, from Asim ibn Sulayman, from Ikrimah: when there is between you and what cuts your prayer the measure of a stone, it does not cut your prayer."
"2233","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah: when it is next to him, it has not cut your prayer."
"2234","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim: when you are in prayer atop a roof with people passing over you, be where people are not seen when they pass. Sufyan said: what prevents you from seeing them is what screens you."
"2235","From Ibn Jurayj: I said to Ata: I prayed toward a stick whose clear part above the ground is a cubit or more, with thirty men behind me, the row extending from here and here; does it suffice me and them against what cuts prayer? He said: yes. I said: then one passing before them and behind me? He said: it cuts their prayer."
"2236","From al-Thawri: Awn ibn Abi Juhayfah informed us, from his father: I saw Bilal bring out the small spear and plant it before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at al-Batha, and he prayed toward it Zuhr and Asr, while dog, donkey and woman passed beyond it. He informed me from al-Thawri that he said: in this, so he led us in prayer toward it."
"2237","From Yahya ibn al-A'la, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad ibn Yazid: I saw myself in rows behind Umar; he prayed with the small spear before him, while women in litters passed before him, yet that did not cut his prayer."
"2238","From al-Thawri and Ibn Uyaynah, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad: Umar would sometimes plant the small spear and pray toward it, while women in litters passed before him."
"2239","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar: the sutrah of the imam is the sutrah of those behind him. Abd al-Razzaq said: and I hold this, and it is the matter upon which people are."
"2240","From Ma'mar, from one who heard al-Hasan say: al-Hakam al-Ghifari prayed with his companions having planted a spear before him; a dog or a donkey passed before them, so he turned to his companions and said: it did not cut my prayer, but it cut your prayer, so he repeated the prayer with them."
"2241","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah or al-Hasan or both: when what cuts prayer passes before the people, it cuts the prayer of the first row, and does not cut the rows behind them."
"2242","From Ibn al-Mubarak: Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah narrated to me, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit: al-Hakam al-Ghifari led people in prayer on a journey with a small spear before him; donkeys passed before his companions, so he repeated the prayer with them. They said: he wanted to do as al-Walid ibn Uqbah does - when he led his companions in the morning prayer with four rak'ahs, then said: shall I add for you. He said: I caught up with al-Hakam and mentioned that to him; he halted until the people caught up. He said: I repeated the prayer with you because of the donkeys that passed before you, yet you struck for me the likeness of Ibn Abi Mu'ayt; I ask Allah to make your journey good, to make your arrival good, to aid you against your enemy, and to separate between me and you. He said: they went on and saw in their faces nothing of that except what pleased them; when they finished, he died."
"2243","From Ibn Jurayj: several informed me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while he was praying with people, a young sheep or a kid goat passed to go before him, so he kept drawing near the pillar and drawing near, until he preceded it and pressed his belly to the pillar; it passed between him and the people, and he did not order the people anything. Abd al-Razzaq said: and we hold this."
"2244","From al-Thawri and Malik, from Abu al-Nadr, from Busr ibn Sa'id: Zayd ibn Khalid sent me to Abu Juhaym al-Ansari to ask him what he heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about a man passing before one praying. He said: I heard him say: to stand in his place forty is better for him than to pass before one praying. He said: I do not know whether he said forty years or forty days."
"2245","From Malik, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Ka'b: if the one passing before one praying knew what is upon him, to be swallowed by the earth would be better for him than to pass before one praying."
"2246","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah: Umar ibn al-Khattab said: if the one passing before one praying knew what is upon him, to stand a year would be better for him than that, when there is no sutrah before the praying person."
"2247","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar: do not let anyone pass before you while you pray; if he refuses but to fight you, fight him."
"2248","From Malik, from Nafi': Abdullah ibn Umar, while he prayed, would not let anyone pass before him."
"2249","From Ibn Jurayj: Nafi' informed me that Ibn Umar would not leave anything passing before him while he prayed, nor would he himself pass before men and women."
"2250","From Dawud ibn Qays, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Sa'id al-Khudri: while Abu Sa'id al-Khudri was praying, a youth came wanting to pass close to his sutrah, and the governor of Madinah that day was Marwan. He said: Abu Sa'id pushed him until he threw him down. The youth went until he entered upon Marwan and said: here is a mad old man who pushed me until he threw me down. He said: do you know him? He said: yes. The Ansar used to visit him on Friday. Abu Sa'id visited him, and Marwan said to the youth: do you know him? He said: yes, is this the old man? Marwan said to the youth: do you know who this is? He said: no. He said: this is a Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Marwan welcomed him and brought him near until he sat close to his gathering, and said to him: this youth mentions that you pushed him until you threw him down. He said: I did not do it? He repeated it to him, saying: I only pushed a devil. Then he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: when one wants to pass between you and your sutrah, repel him; if he refuses, push him; if he refuses, fight him, for he is only a devil."
"2251","From Ma'mar, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Abu Sa'id: a relative of Marwan went before Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, so he forbade him and pushed him; he complained to Marwan, who said to Abu Sa'id: what made you do what you did? He said: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered us not to let anyone pass before us; if he refuses, we push him - or like this."
"2252","From al-Thawri, from Asim, from Muhammad ibn Bashir, from Abu al-Aliyah, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri: a man of Banu Marwan passed before him while he was in prayer, so he pushed him three times. He complained to Marwan, who mentioned that to him. He said: had he refused, I would have seized his hair."
"2253","From Ibn Jurayj: I heard Sulayman ibn Musa narrate, from Ata: Dawud ibn Marwan wanted to pass before Abu Sa'id while he prayed, wearing a robe, Marwan then being governor of the people in Madinah; he repelled him, and as if he refused, he struck his chest. The youth went to his father and told him; Marwan summoned Abu Sa'id, thinking he had struck him because of his robe. He mentioned that. He said: yes; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: repel him; if he refuses, strive against him."
"2254","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wanted to pray, and they saw a donkey, so they sent a man who turned it back."
"2255","From Ibn Jurayj: Amr ibn Shu'ayb informed me, from Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As: while we were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at part of the upper valley wanting to pray - he had stood and we stood - a donkey came out of the pass of Abu Dabb, the pass of Abu Musa; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) held back and did not utter takbir, and Ya'qub ibn Zam'ah, brother of Banu Asad, went to it until he turned it back."
"2256","From Ma'mar, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from a man of Ta'if: a dog came, while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed Asr with people, to pass before them; a man of the people said: O Allah, restrain it; so the dog died. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished, he said: which of you supplicated against it? The man said: I, O Messenger of Allah. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: had he supplicated against a nation of nations, it would have been answered for him."
"2257","From Ma'mar: one who heard Abu al-A'la ibn Abdullah ibn al-Shikhkhir informed me: I saw Uthman - or he said: Uthman used to pray, warding off a sheep lest it pass before him."
"2258","From Isra'il, from Simak, from Ma'mar, from Amr ibn Dinar: I passed beside Ibn Umar, and he thought I would pass before him, so he sprang up startling me and moved me aside."
"2259","From Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Amr ibn Dinar: I went to pass before Ibn Umar while he sat praying; he rebuked, and he was severe against whoever passed before him."
"2260","From Isra'il, from Simak ibn Harb, that he heard Jabir ibn Samurah say: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led us in Fajr prayer, and he began reaching with his hands before him while in prayer; the people asked him when he finished. He said: Satan casts sparks of fire upon me to turn me from prayer, so I seized him; had I taken him, he would not have escaped me until tied to a pillar of the mosque pillars, with the children of the people of Madinah looking at him."
"2261","From al-Thawri, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abdullah ibn Shaqiq: Umar ibn al-Khattab passed by a man praying without a sutrah; when he finished, he said: if the passer and the one passed before knew what is upon them, they would not do it."
"2262","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Umarah, from al-Aswad: Abdullah said: whoever of you can avoid passing before him while he prays, let him do so; the one passing before one praying is lesser in reward than the one passed before."
"2263","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from Abu Mijlaz: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a cat, male or female, ahead toward the qiblah."
"2264","From Ma'mar, from a man of Madinah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad, from his father: Ibn Mas'ud said: when one wants to pass before you while you pray, do not let him, for he takes away half your prayer."
"2265","From al-Thawri, from Dawud, from al-Sha'bi: when the passer has passed you in your prayer, do not repel him another time. Abu Bakr said: I narrated it to Ma'mar, and he said: one who saw al-Hasan praying informed me; a man passed before him and he repelled him, though he had already passed."
"2266","From Abdullah ibn Umar: a man passed before Salim ibn Abdullah, so he pulled him after he was about to pass, until he returned."
"2267","From Ibn Jurayj: it was narrated from Umar ibn al-Khattab that he said: do not let him pass before you, for with him is his devil."
"2268","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq: five are of coarseness: that a man prays in the mosque while people pass before him; that he urinates standing; that the prayer is established while he is beside the mosque and he does not answer; that he wipes dust from his face while in prayer before giving salam; and that he eats with one not of his religion."
"2269","From Ibn Jurayj: I said to Ata: what cuts prayer? He said: the menstruating woman and the black dog."
"2270","Ma'mar informed us, from Ali ibn Zayd ibn Jud'an, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr: the black dog cuts prayer; I think he said: and the menstruating woman. I said to Abu Dharr: why the black dog? He said: I had asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that; he said: it is a devil."
"2271","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Ata ibn Yasar: when the praying person does not pray toward a sutrah, there is no sin on you to pass before him."
"2272","Ma'mar informed us, from Abu Harun al-Abdi, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: dog, donkey and woman cut prayer. From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar."
"2273","From Ma'mar: one who heard Ikrimah informed me, saying: dog, pig, Jew, Christian, Magian, and the menstruating woman cut prayer. From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abdullah ibn Abi Yazid, from Ibn Abbas, similar."
"2274","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas: the menstruating woman and the black dog cut prayer."
"2275","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Layth, from Mujahid, from Mu'adh ibn Jabal: the jet-black dog is a devil, and it cuts prayer."
"2276","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah: a woman does not cut a woman's prayer. And Qatadah was asked: does a young girl who has not menstruated cut prayer? He said: no."
"2277","Ibn Jurayj informed us: Abd al-Karim narrated to me, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas: I and al-Fadl ibn Abbas passed before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) riding a she-ass, while he was praying on the day of Arafah, with none between us screening us from him."
"2278","From Ibn Jurayj: Muhammad ibn Umar ibn Ali informed me that al-Fadl ibn Abbas said: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) visited Abbas while we were in a desert of ours; he stood praying - I think he said Asr - with a bitch of ours and a grazing donkey before him, with nothing between him and them screening him from them."
"2279","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydallah ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah, from Ibn Abbas: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the Farewell Hajj - or he said on the day of the Conquest - while he was praying, I and al-Fadl ibn Abbas riding a she-ass; we cut through the row and dismounted from it, then joined the row, and the she-ass passed before them and did not cut their prayer."
"2280","From al-Thawri, from Simak ibn Harb, from Ikrimah: Ibn Abbas mentioned what cuts prayer, and it was said to him: woman and dog. Ibn Abbas said: to Him ascends the good word and the righteous deed raises it, so what cuts this?"
"2281","From al-Thawri and Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali: nothing cuts prayer; ward off from yourself what you can."
"2282","From Ibn Jurayj: a man wanted to pass before Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, so he took him to Uthman, who said to the man: what would harm you had you turned back when he turned you? Then he turned to Humayd and said to him: what would harm you had he passed before you? Prayer is cut by nothing except speech and ritual impurity. Abd al-Razzaq said: Ibn Jurayj mentioned it from Muhammad ibn Yusuf, from Ibrahim ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf."
"2283","From Isra'il, from Isa ibn Abi Azzah: I heard Amir al-Sha'bi say: nothing cuts prayer. And sometimes I saw a man I forbade to pass before Amir while he prayed; he takes his hand and walks him before him."
"2284","From one whom Ibn Salih heard, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father: nothing cuts prayer except disbelief in Allah; neither man nor woman nor donkey cuts it, except that a man is disliked to walk before him."
"2285","From Ibrahim, from Hammad, from Ibrahim: A'ishah said: you likened me, O people of Iraq, to the dog and the donkey; nothing cuts prayer, but ward off what you can."
"2286","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar: nothing cuts prayer; ward off what you can - or he said what you can."
"2287","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyab: nothing cuts prayer; ward off what you can. He said: nothing cuts prayer; ward off what you can."
"2288","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar: nothing cuts prayer; ward off what you can. He said: and he would not pray except toward a sutrah."
"2289","From Ibrahim ibn Yazid, from al-Walid ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Mughith, from Jabir ibn Abdullah: nothing cuts the Muslim's prayer; ward off what you can."
"2290","From Ma'mar and Ibn Uyaynah, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari: I asked Ibn al-Musayyab what cuts prayer? He said: nothing cuts it except ritual impurity."
"2291","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin: I said to Abidah: what cuts prayer? He said: immorality cuts it, and righteousness completes it, and the like of the back of a saddle suffices you. From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Ibn Sirin, from Abidah, similar."
"2292","From Ibn Jurayj: Ata informed me, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, that A'ishah informed him, saying: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray while I lay across on the bed between him and the qiblah. I said: was the wall of the mosque between them? He said: no, except she was in the house toward its walls."
"2293","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from A'ishah: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray while I lay across between him and the qiblah like the lying of a funeral. From Ibn Uyaynah, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from A'ishah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar."
"2294","From Malik, from Abu al-Nadr, from Abu Salamah, from A'ishah: I would sleep before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with my legs in his qiblah; when he wanted to prostrate he nudged me and I drew in my legs, and when he stood I stretched them. She said: there were no lamps in the houses that day."
"2295","From al-Thawri, from Talhah ibn Yahya, from Ubaydallah ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah, from A'ishah: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed wearing a murayt of these striped murhal garments, part of it on me and part on him; and the mirt is of black woolen cloaks, meaning the striped murhal ones."
"2296","From Malik, from Amir ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from Amr ibn Sulaym: I heard Abu Qatadah say: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray carrying his daughter's daughter Umamah on his shoulder."
"2297","From Ibn Jurayj: Amir ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr informed me that Amr ibn Sulaym al-Zuraqi informed him that he heard Abu Qatadah say: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray with Umamah bint Zaynab bint the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - she being the daughter of Abu al-As ibn al-Rabi' ibn Abd al-Uzza - on his neck; when he bowed he put her down, and when he rose from prostration he took her and returned her on his neck. Amir said: I did not ask him which prayer it was. From Ibn Jurayj: I was informed from Zayd ibn Abi Attab, from Amr ibn Sulaym, that it was the Subh prayer."
"2298","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to take Husayn in prayer and keep him standing until when he prostrated he put him down. I said: in the obligatory? He said: I do not know."
"2299","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to prostrate, and Husayn would climb on his back; when he raised his head he moved him back, and when he prostrated he returned and climbed on his back. He said: when he raised his head he moved him back."
"2300","From Ibn Jurayj: Muhammad ibn Umar ibn Ali and Ja'far ibn Muhammad informed me, both saying: when the prayer was established, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), al-Hasan and al-Husayn and Umamah came rushing to him; when he sat they sat in his lap and on his back, and when he stood he put them down likewise, thus until his prayer finished."
"2301","From Malik: it reached me that a man brought to Uthman ibn Affan a man whose nose was broken. He said to him: he passed before me in prayer while I was praying, and what I heard about the one passing before one praying had reached me. Uthman said to him: you did evil, O son of my brother; you wasted the prayer and broke his nose."
"2302","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father: nothing cuts prayer in Makkah; it does not harm you that a woman passes before you."
"2303","From Ibn Jurayj: my father informed me, from Abu Amir: I saw Ibn al-Zubayr praying in the mosque, and a woman wanted to pass before him while he wanted to prostrate, until when she had passed he prostrated in the place of her feet."
"2304","From Amr ibn Qays: Kathir ibn Kathir ibn al-Muttalib ibn Abi Wada'ah informed me, from his father, from his grandfather: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praying in the Sacred Mosque, with people circumambulating the House between him and the qiblah before him, with no sutrah between him and them."
"2305","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Kathir ibn Kathir, from his father, from his grandfather: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praying in the Sacred Mosque, with people circumambulating the House between him and the qiblah before him, with no sutrah between him and them. From Ibn Uyaynah, from Kathir ibn Kathir, from his father, from his grandfather, similar, except he said: I saw him praying toward the side of the gate of Banu Sahm."
"2306","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar: I saw Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiyyah praying in the mosque of Mina, with people passing before him; a youth of his family came and sat before him. Abd al-Razzaq said: and I saw Ibn Jurayj praying in the mosque of Mina to the left of the minaret, with no sutrah before him; a boy came and sat before him."
"2307","From al-Thawri, from Abu al-A'la Burd ibn Sinan, from Ubadah ibn Nusayy, from Ghudayf ibn al-Harith: I said to the Commander of the Faithful: we go to the desert; if I go out I am alone, and if my wife goes out she is alone. He said: cut between you and her with a cloth, then pray and let her pray - meaning cut in the tent."
"2308","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Abu al-Huwayrith: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray with some of his wives on his right and on his left, while they were menstruating."
"2309","From Ibn Jurayj: Hasan ibn Muslim informed me, saying: a man said: I asked Tawus, saying: what is with people, none remains to pray with a man facing him? He said: no - because of a man who vowed to kiss the forehead of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then Tawus informed the man of that report. Hasan said: I asked Tawus about that and he concealed it from me, saying: you only want to say: Tawus informed me. He said: I instructed a man of the pilgrims, between me and him, saying to him: ask him, was there a man who vowed to kiss the forehead of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He came to prostrate on his forehead? He said: come here; he came until the man faced the qiblah, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the man facing him; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) inclined his head until he enabled him of his forehead, so he prostrated on it, both facing the qiblah, neither of them in prayer. Hasan said: the one who informed him erred, saying: to kiss. And I knew the report was from Tawus, and I knew he dislikes - meaning the prayer of a man with a man facing him - for that."
"2310","From Ibn Jurayj: a man of Banu Khuzaymah informed me that Khuzaymah ibn Thabit vowed to prostrate on the forehead of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disliked it, and the man's self... and this was the report."
"2311","From Abd al-Karim Abu Umayyah, from Tawus: a man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I vowed to prostrate on your face. He faced the qiblah, then the man inclined his head from behind him, and the man prostrated behind the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) facing the qiblah."
"2312","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Shamir ibn Atiyyah, from Hilal ibn Yasaf: Umar saw a man praying with a man facing him; he turned to this one with the stick, saying: you pray with this one facing you? And he turned to this one with the stick, saying: do you face him while he prays?"
"2313","From Ibn Jurayj: I said to Ata: was wiping dust for the face forbidden? He said: yes, and it is said: when you see something you dislike, delay it. I said: what thing? He said: we heard that, and dearer to me that you not wipe it. I said: what if I wiped? He said: do not repeat, and do not prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness."
"2314","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abu Dharr: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: when one of you stands to prayer, mercy faces him, so do not move the pebbles."
"2315","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, that Abu al-Ahwas narrated to him that he heard Abu Dharr say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: when one of you stands for prayer, mercy faces him, so let him not wipe the pebbles."
"2316","From Ibn Jurayj: Ma'mar and Ibn Dinar informed me, from a man he named, from Abu Dharr, that he said: whoever comes to witness the prayer and it is established while he is on the road, let him not hasten, nor increase over the manner of his first walking; what he catches let him pray with the imam, and what he misses let him complete; and let him not wipe his face when he prays; if he wipes then once, and to refrain from it is better for him than a hundred black-eyed she-camels."
"2317","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, raised to Abu Dharr: concession in one wipe for prostration, and leaving it is better than a hundred black-eyed she-camels."
"2318","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from a man of Banu Ghifar, from Abu Basrah, from Abu Dharr: when the prayer draws near, walk in your manner; pray what you catch and complete what preceded you; do not wipe the earth except one wipe, and to refrain from it is better for you than a hundred she-camels, all black-eyed."
"2319","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Isa, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Abu Dharr: I asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about everything until I asked him about wiping the pebbles. He said: once, or leave."
"2320","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Abi Najih: Mujahid said, Abu Dharr said: I asked my intimate friend about everything until wiping the pebbles? He said: once."
"2321","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Muhammad ibn Talhah and Abdullah ibn Ayyash ibn Abi Rabiah, he said: Abu Dharr passed by me while I was praying, and said: The ground is wiped only a single wipe."
"2322","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about smoothing the pebbles in prayer, and he said: If you must do it, then once."
"2323","From Israil, from Abu Ishaq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd, he said: Abdullah ibn Zayd used to level the pebbles with his hand a single time when he wanted to prostrate, and he would say in his prostration: At Your service, O Allah, at Your service, and ready to serve You."
"2324","From Malik, from his uncle Ibn Abi Suhayl, from his father, he said: I was with Uthman when the prayer started while I was speaking to him about assigning me a stipend, and I kept speaking to him while he leveled the pebbles with his hand, until men came who had all finished straightening the rows and informed him they were straight; then he said to me: Take your place in the row, then say the takbir."
"2325","From Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, he said: Tawus used to wipe the dust away from his face with one wipe when he wanted to prostrate; and Ibn Jurayj also mentioned it from Tawus."
"2326","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: May I prostrate on a stone that hurts my face? He said: Throw it aside and prostrate with your face until it rests on the ground, or around your face."
"2327","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard a man turning over pebbles in prayer in the mosque, and when he finished, he said: Who was turning over the pebbles in prayer? The man said: Me, O Messenger of Allah. He said: That is your share of your prayer."
"2328","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Talhah ibn Musarrif, he said: Turning over pebbles in the mosque is an annoyance to the angel; from Ibn al-Taymi, from Layth, the same."
"2329","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: They used to be strict about wiping the pebbles from the place of the forehead in a way they were not strict about wiping dust from the face? He said: Yes, by Allah, indeed."
"2330","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: I shook my hands free of dust before finishing the prayer? He said: I do not like that."
"2331","From Mamar, from Qatadah: that he used to wipe his forehead when he finished the prayer, before saying the salam; from Mamar, he said: I sometimes saw al-Zuhri doing it."
"2332","From a shaykh of the people of al-Jazirah called Ibn Ullathah, he said: It was deemed good for a man, when he finished his prayer, to wipe the dust from his face, then say: In the name of Allah besides Whom there is no god, Knower of the unseen and the seen; O Allah, remove grief from me."
"2333","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: It is said: If you can avoid wiping the dust from your face until you finish your prayer, do so, and if you wipe there is no harm; and it is more beloved to me that you not wipe until you finish. Ata said: I do all of that: sometimes I wipe before finishing my prayer, and sometimes I do not wipe until I finish my prayer."
"2334","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if I wiped my face after saying: Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah, and the tashahhud, before the imam says the salam? He said: It will not harm you."
"2335","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: It is more beloved to me that you not wipe until you finish."
"2336","From Mamar, from a man who heard Maymun ibn Mihran: he disliked that a man wipe his face of dust during prayer; he said: I mentioned that to al-Hasan, who used to wipe his face before saying the salam, and he said: Should I leave the dust on my face?"
"2337","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was told that they did not form rows until there was revealed: And indeed we are those who stand in rows (165), and indeed we are those who glorify (166)."
"2338","From Mamar, from Hammam ibn Munabbih, that he heard Abu Hurayrah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Straighten the rows, for straightening the rows is part of the excellence of prayer."
"2339","From Mamar, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Part of the perfection of prayer is straightening the row. Mamar informed us, from Qatadah, from Anas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"2340","Mamar informed us, from Thabit, from Anas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Keep watch over these rows, for I see you from behind me."
"2341","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi informed me that Ibn Umar used to say: Part of the perfection of prayer is straightening the row."
"2342","From al-Thawri, from Simak ibn Harb, from al-Numan ibn Bashir, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to straighten us in prayer as one straightens arrows; he did that with us repeatedly, until when he saw that we had learned, he stepped forward and saw the chest of a man sticking out, and said: O servants of Allah, you will straighten your rows, or Allah will set your faces against one another."
"2343","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Umarah ibn Umayr, from Abu Mamar al-Azdi, from Abu Masud al-Ansari, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to wipe our shoulders in prayer and say: Do not differ, or your hearts will differ; let those of understanding and reason be closest to me, then those next to them, then those next to them. Abu Masud said: Today you differ even more."
"2344","From Mamar, from Mansur, from Talhah al-Yami, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awsajah, from al-Bara, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to wipe our chests in prayer from here to here, saying: Straighten your rows; do not differ, or your hearts will differ. Allah and His angels send blessings upon the first row - or he said: the rows."
"2345","Whoever grants a loan of silver or milk, or gives a leather water-skin as a gift, it equals freeing a slave."
"2346","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Musayyab ibn Rafi, from Tamim al-Tai, from Jabir ibn Samurah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Will you not stand in rows behind me as the angels stand in rows before their Lord? They said: And how do the angels stand in rows before their Lord? He said: They complete the front rows and stand pressed together in the row."
"2347","From Mamar, from Aban ibn Abi Ayyash, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, he said: We used to pray with Umar, and he would say: Fill your rows so your shoulders meet; let Satan not get between you, as if they are small lambs."
"2348","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Press together in the row, or the young of hadhaf from Satan will get between you; for Allah and His angels send blessings upon those who straighten the rows."
"2349","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Umarah ibn Imran al-Jafi, from Suwayd ibn Ghafalah, he said: Bilal used to strike our feet in prayer and level our shoulders."
"2350","From al-Thawri, from Asim, from Abu Uthman, he said: I saw Umar, when he stepped forward to prayer, look at the shoulders and the feet."
"2351","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi, the freedman of Ibn Umar, informed me, he said: Umar used to send a man to straighten the rows, then he would not say the takbir until he came to him and informed him that the rows were straight."
"2352","From Nafi: Umar ibn al-Khattab used to order the straightening of the rows, and when they came and informed him they were straight, he would say the takbir."
"2353","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: Umar would not say the takbir until the rows were straight; he appointed men for that."
"2354","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Hasan ibn Muslim informed me, from one of his companions, from Uthman ibn Affan, that he used to say: Straighten your rows, align the shoulders, help your slaves, and restrain yourselves; for the believer restrains himself and helps his slave, while the hypocrite does not help his slave nor restrain himself. And do not burden the boy who is not a craftsman with tax, for if he does not find his tax he will steal; and do not burden the slave-girl who is not skilled with tax, for if she finds nothing she will seek it with her private parts."
"2355","From Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, and from Musa ibn Uqbah: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: Straighten the rows, align the shoulders, and listen attentively; for the reward of the attentive one who does not hear is like the reward of the attentive one who hears."
"2356","From Hisham, from Malik ibn Abi Amir, from Uthman ibn Affan, that he used to say in his sermon, rarely omitting to preach it: When the imam stands, listen and be silent, for the silent one who does not hear has a share like the one who hears; and when the prayer is established, straighten the rows and align with the shoulders, for straightening the row is part of the perfection of prayer. Then he would not say the takbir until men he had appointed to straighten the rows came to him and informed him they were straight, then he would say the takbir."
"2357","From Dawud ibn Qays, from Dawud ibn Husayn, the freedman of Umar, he said: Uthman used to say: Straighten the rows, align the feet, keep shoulders level, and hear and be silent; for the silent one who does not hear has the same as the silent one who hears."
"2358","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I saw the guards of one of the rulers of Makkah ordering the straightening of the rows while not praying with the people, so I said to Ata: Did that please you from the guards? He said: No, by Allah, not until they pray with the people. Glory be to Allah."
"2359","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What about a man leaving the row when they sit in the last tashahhud, making room in the row? He said: I do not like it to be except after the taslim; and it is more beloved to me that he remain firm; and if he makes room because of crowding, there is no harm after the taslim either."
"2360","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn al-Munkadir: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the one who leaves the rows: That is the sitting place of Satan; and the one who raises his head before the imam, he said: His head is reined by the hand of Satan, who raises it and lowers it."
"2361","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: People crowd after the imam has said the takbir? He said: No, except that one walks holding the hand of another among the people, leaving it for the row behind him, excused, walking behind him? He said: There is no harm in that. I said: He leaves turning his back to the qiblah facing the row behind him? He said: I do not like that. I said: And he does not prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness? He said: No, he only turns away fearing he may collide with a person."
"2362","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to him: Is it disliked for a man to walk breaking through the rows after the imam has said the takbir? He said: No, except that he walk in front of someone. Then he said afterwards: If he breaks through the rows to a gap he has done well, and it is due upon the people to press the rows together so there are no gaps between them. Then he said: Allah loves those who fight in His cause in a row as though they are a solid structure, so prayer is more deserving of that."
"2363","From Mamar, from Mansur, from Talhah al-Yami, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awsajah, from al-Bara ibn Azib, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah and His angels send blessings upon the first row."
"2364","From Israil, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay, from Abu Salih, and from Ali ibn Rabiah, they said: The Messenger of Allah prayed the Isha prayer, then said: Allah and His angels send blessings upon the foremost row."
"2365","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me that he heard Yahya ibn Jadah saying: The rows most entitled to completion are the first of them; Allah and His angels send blessings upon the first row."
"2366","Mamar and Ikrimah ibn Ammar informed us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Harith al-Taymi, from Khalid ibn Madan, from Irbad ibn Sariyah: The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to seek forgiveness for the foremost first row three times, and for the second once."
"2367","Ikrimah ibn Ammar informed us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Aishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: People will keep staying back from the first row until Allah puts them back into the Fire."
"2368","From Ikrimah ibn Ammar, or Umar ibn Rashid, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from a man, from Abdullah ibn Shaddad, that Ibn Masud said: Allah and His angels send blessings upon those who advance before the rows in their prayer, meaning the foremost row."
"2369","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Yazid, he said: I saw al-Miswar ibn Makhramah slipping through the rows until he reached the first and the second."
"2370","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Umarah, from Abu Mamar, from Abu Masud: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: Let those of understanding and reason be closest to me, then those next to them."
"2371","From Abdullah ibn Umar, and al-Thawri, from Humayd, from Anas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) liked that the Emigrants and the Helpers stand closest to him in prayer."
"2372","From al-Thawri, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from a man, from Uthman: Umar used to order the straightening of the rows, then say: Step forward O so-and-so, step forward O so-and-so, step back O so-and-so. Sufyan said: He would put forward their righteous ones and put back the others."
"2373","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, he said: Umar used to order the straightening of the rows and say: Step forward O so-and-so; I think he said: People will keep staying back until Allah puts them back."
"2374","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, from Khalid, from Qays ibn Ubad, he said: When I came to Madinah I entered the mosque for the Asr prayer and stepped forward to the first row, then a man came, took me by the shoulder, moved me back, and stood in my place after the imam had said the takbir and I had said it. When we finished the prayer he turned to me and said: I only moved you back because the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered us that the Emigrants and Helpers pray in the first row, so I knew you were not among them and moved you back. I said: Who is this? They said: Ubayy ibn Kab."
"2375","From Ibn Uyaynah, from a man among them, he said: Hudhayfah saw a man in the first row and moved him back, saying: You are not among them."
"2376","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Humayd al-Tawil, from Anas, he said: When the prayer was established, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would stand in his place of prayer, then turn to the people and say: Straighten your rows, for I see you from behind me."
"2377","From Mamar, from Thabit, from Anas: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say when he stood: Keep watch over these rows, for I see you from behind me."
"2378","From Ibn Uyaynah, from al-Hasan ibn Ubaydullah, he said: I heard Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, who used to lead us; when he stood to lead us he said: Straighten the rows, for part of the perfection of prayer is straightening the row."
"2379","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Did the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) himself straighten the rows of the people from behind them? He said: I have heard it. He said: So I thought: Is it upon the imams to order their guards to do that, straightening the people? He said: Yes. Then he said: Rather they are ordered; it sufficed them that the people in that time were few, new to disbelief, so they were being taught."
"2380","From Ibn Jurayj, Ata informed me, he said: When the people were few, Ibn al-Zubayr would place them behind the Maqam; that was criticized against him. A man said to Ata: What if there were, behind the Maqam, those whom, if he placed them around the House, they would circumambulate around it in a row, but there is a gap in it - which of those is more beloved to you? He said: As for him - seeing the angels surrounding the Throne - as if saying: their encircling is their rows around the House, more beloved to me."
"2381","From al-Thawri, from Amr ibn Qays, and Hammad, or one of them, from Ibrahim: that he disliked that a man stand in the second row until the first row is complete, and disliked standing in the third row until the second is complete; and the imam should order them to do that."
"2382","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever releases a Muslim from a sale, Allah will release his soul on the Day of Resurrection; and whoever joins a row, Allah will join his footsteps on the Day of Resurrection."
"2383","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Harun ibn Abi Aishah informed me, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever joins a row in the cause of Allah, or in prayer, Allah will join his footsteps on the Day of Resurrection; and whoever releases a regretful one, Allah will release his soul on the Day of Resurrection."
"2384","Al-Thawri informed us, from Usamah ibn Zayd, from Abdullah ibn Urwah, from Urwah, from Aishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah and His angels send blessings upon the one who prays in the first row."
"2385","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Layth, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar: No man takes a step of greater reward than a step he takes to a gap in a row to fill it."
"2386","From Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Rawwad, I heard him saying: Ibn Umar said: For my front teeth to fall is more beloved to me than to see a gap in the row ahead of me and not join it."
"2387","From Yahya ibn al-Ala, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Salih ibn Kaysan: Ibn Umar said: For my front teeth to fall out is more beloved to me than to see a breach in the row and not fill it."
"2388","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: It has reached us that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: Beware of gaps, meaning in the row. Ata said: It has reached us that when Satan finds a gap he enters it."
"2389","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: He informed me that Ibn Umar used to order us that there be no gaps between the rows."
"2390","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: I am between two men, with a gap between me and each of them; shall I stick to one of them or stand evenly between them? He said: Stand evenly between them, unless what is between your knees is close, then stick them together. I said: I find rows cut off; is it not most right that I join the one nearest to me of the congregation? He said: Yes."
"2391","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: More than one informed me, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Keep to the right sides of the rows, beware of what is between the pillars, and keep to the first row."
"2392","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Misar, from Adi ibn Thabit, from al-Bara ibn Azib, he said: I like to pray to the right of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), because when he said the salam he would turn to us with his face - or he said: he would begin us with the salam."
"2393","From Mamar, he said: One who saw al-Hasan and Ibn Sirin informed me that they prayed on the left side of the mosque, because their dwellings were on that side; he said: And I saw Mamar praying on the left side of the mosque."
"2394","From Mamar, from Zayd ibn Aslam, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best of you are the gentlest of you in shoulders in prayer."
"2395","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Is it disliked for a man to stand alone behind the row? He said: Yes, and two men and three, except in the row, for there are gaps in it. I said to Ata: What if I find the row pressed together and see no gap - shall I stand behind them? He said: Allah does not burden a soul beyond its capacity; and it is more beloved to me, by Allah, that I enter into it."
"2396","Ibn Jurayj mentioned, from Abd al-Karim Abu Umayyah, from Ibrahim, he said: It is said: If the row is pressed together with no entrance in it, let him draw out a man from that row to stand with him; and if he does not do so, that prayer of his is the prayer of an individual, not congregational prayer."
"2397","Al-Thawri informed us, from Mamar, from Mansur, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Ziyad ibn Abi al-Jad, from Wabisah ibn Mabad, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw a man praying alone behind the row and ordered him to repeat the prayer."
"2398","From Uthman ibn Matar, from Said ibn Abi Arubah, from Abu Mashar, from Ibrahim, about a man who finds the row level: he said: He draws back a man; and if he does not do so, his prayer is not valid."
"2399","From Abdullah ibn Kathir, from Shubah ibn al-Hajjaj, he said: I asked al-Hakam ibn Utaybah and Hammad about that; al-Hakam said: He repeats, and Hammad: He does not repeat."
"2400","From Ibn Uyaynah, he said: I heard him mention, from one of them, that Ibrahim said: If he stood level with the imam, he does not repeat."
"2401","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: He does not repeat."
"2402","From Mamar, from Abu Ishaq, from Mad Yakrib, he said: Ibn Masud said: Do not form rows between the pillars, and do not follow in prayer people who are talking."
"2403","From al-Thawri, and Ibn Uyaynah, from Abu Ishaq, from Madi Karib al-Hamdani, he said: I heard Ibn Masud saying: Do not line up between the columns, and do not pray with people arguing before you - or he said: engaged in idle talk."
"2404","From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Hani, he said: Abd al-Hamid ibn Mahmud told me, he said: I was with Anas ibn Malik, and we stood between the pillars, so we moved back; and when we had prayed, Anas said: We used to avoid this in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"2405","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from al-Hasan: that he disliked the row between the pillars; Hisham said: I asked Ibn Sirin about it, and he saw no harm in it."
"2406","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abd al-Karim ibn Abi al-Mukhariq, from Mujahid, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I was forbidden to pray behind sleepers and talkers."
"2407","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me, he said: I prayed behind Abu Hurayrah and heard him say the takbir when he began, when he bowed, when he bent down for prostration, then when he raised his head, then when he bent his head, then when he bent his head to prostrate the second time, then when he raised his head, then when he stood up straight from two units. He said to me: Such is the takbir in every prayer."
"2408","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if I did not finish the takbirah until I place my forehead on the ground? He said: It is more beloved to me that you finish it before your forehead lands."
"2409","From Dawud ibn Qays, from Maymun ibn Maysarah, he said: I prayed with Abu Hurayrah and he used to say the takbir with us - meaning the takbir when bowing and when prostrating."
"2410","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: Abu Hurayrah used to say the takbir with us: saying it when he stood, when he bowed, when he intended to prostrate, after finishing the prostration, when he sat, when he intended to stand in the two units saying the takbir, and the same in the other two units; and when he said the salam, he said: By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, I am the closest of you in resemblance to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - meaning in prayer; this remained his prayer until he left the world."
"2411","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab informed us, from Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham, that he heard Abu Hurayrah saying: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood for prayer, he would say the takbir when he stood, say the takbir when he bowed, then say: Allah hears the one who praises Him, when raising his spine from the bow; then say while standing: Our Lord, to You is praise; then say the takbir when going down prostrating, then say the takbir when raising his head, then do that in all the prayers until completing them; and say the takbir when standing from the pair after sitting. Then Abu Hurayrah would say: I am the closest of you in prayer to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"2412","From Malik, from Ibn Shihab, from Ali ibn al-Husayn: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say the takbir every time he lowered and raised, and that remained his prayer until he met Allah."
"2413","Mamar informed us, from Qatadah and others, from Mutarrif ibn Abdullah ibn al-Shikhkhir, he said: Imran ibn Husayn and I prayed in Kufah behind Ali ibn Abi Talib; he said this takbir when bowing and when prostrating, saying all of it. When we finished, Imran said to me: I have not prayed for a while - or for such-and-such - resembling the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) more than this prayer, meaning the prayer of Ali."
"2414","From Mamar, from Qatadah, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghunm: Abu Malik al-Ashari said to his people: Gather so I may pray with you the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). When they gathered he said: Is there anyone among you from other than you? They said: No, except a nephew of ours. He said: The nephew of a people is among them. Then he called for a basin with water, washed his hands, rinsed and sniffed, washed his face three times, his forearms three times each, wiped his head, washed his feet, then prayed the Zuhr with them, saying twenty-two takbirs in them: saying the takbir when he prostrated and when he raised his head from prostration, reciting in the first two units the Opening of the Book, letting those next to him hear."
"2415","From Mamar, from Asim ibn Abi al-Najud, from Shaqiq ibn Salamah: Ibn Masud used to say the takbir every time he lowered and raised."
"2416","Al-Thawri informed us, from Abd al-Rahman al-Asamm, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Abu Bakr, Umar and Uthman used to affirm the takbir when they raised and when they lowered."
"2417","From Malik, from Wahb ibn Kaysan, from Jabir ibn Abdullah: that he used to say the takbir every time he lowered and raised."
"2418","From Malik, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim ibn Abdullah: Abdullah ibn Umar used to say the takbir every time he lowered and raised."
"2419","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi informed me that Ibn Umar used to say the takbir in prayer when he began, when he bowed, when he bent down to prostrate before placing his head, when he raised from the prostration, then when he placed returning to prostrate before placing his face, when he raised his head from the prostration, then when he stood up from the pair standing. Ibn Jurayj said: And Tawus used to say: Such was the prayer."
"2420","From Israil, from Furat, he said: I asked Said ibn Jubayr about the takbir in prayer? He said: Complete the takbir."
